  I’m figuring out how to implement storage usage effectively. I want to store ''packing_utils.py'' and ''README.md'', but saving ''best_centers_radii.npz'' needs the content string. I can store text values for npz, yet must remain cautious about reading from the proper lineage. I plan to create and write modules in ''storage/verity'' for reuse, allowing us to warm start. It seems fine to utilize storage modules, but I need to be mindful of the code length. I’ll also check storage info and prepare for writing ''lp_dual_grad.py'' containing functions for the gradient approach. Let’s create these modules progressively, keeping the future token budget in mind.**Defining gradient optimization and module creation**


  I''m laying out the plan for the optimization process and module creation. I need to calculate gradients carefully near boundaries, ensuring centers stay within the (0,1) box. The loop for center optimization will compute the LP solution, update centers based on gradients, and handle annealing. I’m planning a function to load past best centers and save new results after processing. The implementation needs to keep the code efficient and not exceed the limit of concurrent experiments. I’ll construct the ''lp_dual_grad.py'' module and prepare for submission while managing the token budget wisely.**Implementing dual-guided optimization**


  I’m outlining the dual-guided gradient center optimizer. First, we need to gracefully access attributes, defaulting pair_duals to zero if they''re not present. I’ll implement various functions including one to compute gradients, another for boundary conditions, and a final one to refine center positions. The script will also ensure persistence by saving results. It’s crucial to import the necessary libraries and manage the initial conditions effectively, using cached values when possible. Now I’ll write the code from this plan into the module and prepare for the next submission.**Writing optimization functions**


  I''m implementing core functions for the dual-guided optimizer, starting with pair indices and boundary margins. The `solve_lp` function assembles the linear programming problem, extracts marginals, and handles potential errors gracefully. I''ll define gradient functions for boundary and pair duals, along with methods for initializing centers and refining their positions within a time budget. It’s crucial to ensure the results are saved and accessible. Lastly, I''ll prepare the main execution script to put everything into action, but I might wait to avoid overloading the system with concurrent trials.**Crafting YAML content**


  I need to adhere to the important YAML rule about enclosing single-line text values with special characters in quotes. I’ll ensure there''s no colon in the title and that the abstract will be enclosed in quotes as well. I’ll set the tags like "circle packing, linear programming, duals, gradient ascent, persistence." Next, I’ll prepare the ''storage write'' YAML content using a block-style format. I might also want to request a ''rank score'' for the scoreboard later, but for now, I’ll focus on the code submission.'
