- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Combinatorial tri-hex pattern search + LP dual-gradient refinement (>=2.6667 fallback, persistence)\"\ntags: \"circle packing, linear programming, dual gradients, hexagonal lattice, randomized search, persistence\"\nabstract: \"We sample many 6- or 7-row tri/hex lattice patterns whose row-counts sum to 32, build layouts with hex-consistent spacings, and rank by a HiGHS LP objective. We refine the top candidates via LP dual-gradient ascent with Armijo backtracking, interleaved with micro-jitter and targeted worst-circle relocation. We persist the best result and always fall back to the 6×6-minus-corners r=1/12 configuration if needed.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import os\n  import time\n\n  try:\n      from scipy.optimize import linprog\n      SCIPY_AVAILABLE = True\n  except Exception:\n      SCIPY_AVAILABLE = False\n\n  # ---------------- Basic utilities ----------------\n\n  def _pairwise_indices(n: int):\n      I = []\n\
    \      J = []\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              I.append(i); J.append(j)\n      return np.array(I, dtype=int), np.array(J, dtype=int)\n\n  def _boundary_margins(C: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      x = C[:, 0]; y = C[:, 1]\n      return np.minimum(np.minimum(x, 1.0 - x), np.minimum(y, 1.0 - y))\n\n  def _clip_box(C: np.ndarray, margin: float = 1e-6) -> np.ndarray:\n      return np.clip(C, margin, 1.0 - margin)\n\n  # ---------------- LP for radii (+duals) ----------------\n\n  def _solve_lp(C: np.ndarray):\n      \"\"\"\n      Maximize sum r_i s.t. 0 <= r_i <= b_i and r_i + r_j <= ||c_i - c_j|| for all i<j.\n      Returns: r, obj, ok, y_pairs, u_upper, I, J\n      \"\"\"\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      if n != 32 or C.shape != (32, 2):\n          return np.zeros(32), 0.0, False, None, None, None, None\n      b = _boundary_margins(C)\n      I, J = _pairwise_indices(n)\n\n      if not SCIPY_AVAILABLE:\n          r = np.minimum(b, 1.0/12.0) * 0.999\n\
    \          return r, float(np.sum(r)), True, None, None, I, J\n\n      D = np.linalg.norm(C[I] - C[J], axis=1)\n      m = len(I)\n      A_ub = np.zeros((m, n), dtype=float)\n      A_ub[np.arange(m), I] = 1.0\n      A_ub[np.arange(m), J] = 1.0\n      b_ub = D\n\n      c = -np.ones(n, dtype=float)\n      bounds = [(0.0, float(max(0.0, bi))) for bi in b]\n\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      if not hasattr(res, \"success\") or not res.success:\n          return np.zeros(n), 0.0, False, None, None, I, J\n\n      r = res.x.astype(float)\n      obj = float(-res.fun)\n\n      y_pairs = None\n      u_upper = None\n      try:\n          if hasattr(res, \"ineqlin\") and hasattr(res.ineqlin, \"marginals\") and res.ineqlin.marginals is not None:\n              y_pairs = np.maximum(0.0, np.asarray(res.ineqlin.marginals, dtype=float))\n          if hasattr(res, \"upper\") and hasattr(res.upper, \"marginals\") and res.upper.marginals is not None:\n\
    \              u_upper = np.maximum(0.0, np.asarray(res.upper.marginals, dtype=float))\n      except Exception:\n          y_pairs = None\n          u_upper = None\n\n      if y_pairs is None:\n          slack = D - (r[I] + r[J])\n          y_pairs = 1.0 / (1e-6 + np.maximum(slack, 0.0))\n          s = np.sum(y_pairs)\n          if s > 0:\n              y_pairs *= (n / s)\n      if u_upper is None:\n          tight = (b - r) < 5e-4\n          u_upper = tight.astype(float)\n\n      return r, obj, True, y_pairs, u_upper, I, J\n\n  # ---------------- Gradients from duals ----------------\n\n  def _grad_from_duals(C: np.ndarray, y_pairs, I, J, eps: float = 1e-12):\n      G = np.zeros_like(C)\n      V = C[I] - C[J]\n      d = np.linalg.norm(V, axis=1)\n      d = np.maximum(d, eps)\n      U = (V.T / d).T\n      W = y_pairs.reshape(-1, 1)\n      contrib = U * W\n      np.add.at(G, I, contrib)\n      np.add.at(G, J, -contrib)\n      return G\n\n  def _grad_boundary(C: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray,\
    \ u_upper, weight: float = 0.12):\n      G = np.zeros_like(C)\n      w = np.maximum(0.0, u_upper)\n      for i in range(C.shape[0]):\n          if w[i] <= 0:\n              continue\n          x, y = C[i]\n          ds = np.array([x, 1.0 - x, y, 1.0 - y], dtype=float)\n          k = int(np.argmin(ds))\n          if   k == 0: v = np.array([ 1.0,  0.0])\n          elif k == 1: v = np.array([-1.0,  0.0])\n          elif k == 2: v = np.array([ 0.0,  1.0])\n          else:        v = np.array([ 0.0, -1.0])\n          G[i] += weight * w[i] * v\n      return G\n\n  # ---------------- Validation and shrink ----------------\n\n  def _validate(C: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray) -> bool:\n      if C.shape != (32, 2) or r.shape != (32,):\n          return False\n      if np.any(~np.isfinite(C)) or np.any(~np.isfinite(r)) or np.any(r <= 0):\n          return False\n      x = C[:, 0]; y = C[:, 1]\n      if np.any(x - r < 0) or np.any(x + r > 1) or np.any(y - r < 0) or np.any(y + r > 1):\n          return\
    \ False\n      diff = C[:, None, :] - C[None, :, :]\n      D = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff * diff, axis=-1))\n      R = r[:, None] + r[None, :]\n      iu = np.triu_indices(32, 1)\n      if np.any(D[iu] < R[iu] - 1e-12):\n          return False\n      return True\n\n  def _safety_shrink(C: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray, factor: float = 0.9995) -> np.ndarray:\n      r2 = r.astype(float).copy() * factor\n      r2 = np.minimum(r2, _boundary_margins(C))\n      r2 = np.maximum(r2, 1e-12)\n      return r2\n\n  # ---------------- Layout builders ----------------\n\n  def _baseline_grid_6x6_minus_corners():\n      s = 1.0 / 6.0\n      r0 = 0.5 * s\n      xs = r0 + s * np.arange(6, dtype=float)\n      ys = r0 + s * np.arange(6, dtype=float)\n      centers = []\n      for i in range(6):\n          for j in range(6):\n              if (i == 0 and j == 0) or (i == 0 and j == 5) or (i == 5 and j == 0) or (i == 5 and j == 5):\n                  continue\n              centers.append([xs[j], ys[i]])\n      C =\
    \ np.array(centers, dtype=float)\n      r = np.full(32, r0 * (1.0 - 1e-12), dtype=float)\n      return C, r, float(np.sum(r))\n\n  def _tri_hex_layout(row_counts, y_margin: float = 0.09, x_margin: float = None, fudge_x: float = 1.0):\n      rows = len(row_counts)\n      if x_margin is None:\n          x_margin = y_margin\n      Hx = 1.0 - 2.0 * x_margin\n      Hy = 1.0 - 2.0 * y_margin\n      ys = np.array([0.5]) if rows <= 1 else np.linspace(y_margin, 1.0 - y_margin, rows)\n      s_y = 0.0 if rows <= 1 else ys[1] - ys[0]\n      s_x_hex = 2.0 * s_y / np.sqrt(3) if s_y > 0 else Hx / max(1, (max(row_counts) - 1))\n      s_x_fit = Hx / max(1, (max(row_counts) - 1))\n      s_x = fudge_x * min(s_x_hex if s_x_hex > 0 else s_x_fit, s_x_fit)\n      k_max = max(row_counts)\n      C = []\n      for idx, k in enumerate(row_counts):\n          if k <= 1:\n              xs = np.array([0.5], dtype=float)\n          else:\n              width = (k - 1) * s_x\n              x0 = x_margin + 0.5 * (Hx\
    \ - width)\n              offset = 0.5 * s_x if k < k_max else 0.0\n              if x0 + offset < x_margin or x0 + offset + width > 1.0 - x_margin:\n                  offset = 0.0\n              xs = x0 + offset + s_x * np.arange(k, dtype=float)\n          for x in xs:\n              C.append([x, ys[idx]])\n      return _clip_box(np.array(C, dtype=float))\n\n  def _farthest_sampling(n: int = 32, margin: float = 0.02, candidates: int = 500, seed: int = 0):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      C = [np.array([0.5, 0.5], dtype=float)]\n      for _ in range(1, n):\n          cand = rng.uniform(margin, 1.0 - margin, size=(candidates, 2))\n          exist = np.array(C, dtype=float)\n          d2 = np.sum((cand[:, None, :] - exist[None, :, :]) ** 2, axis=-1)\n          d = np.sqrt(d2, out=np.zeros_like(d2), where=(d2 > 0))\n          min_to_pts = np.min(d, axis=1)\n          b = np.minimum(np.minimum(cand[:, 0], 1.0 - cand[:, 0]),\n                         np.minimum(cand[:, 1],\
    \ 1.0 - cand[:, 1]))\n          score = np.minimum(min_to_pts, 2.0 * b)\n          C.append(cand[int(np.argmax(score))])\n      return _clip_box(np.array(C, dtype=float))\n\n  # ---------------- Pattern generation ----------------\n\n  def _make_pattern(L: int, target: int = 32, low: int = 4, high: int = 6, rng=None):\n      if rng is None:\n          rng = np.random.default_rng()\n      counts = [5] * L\n      diff = target - 5 * L\n      tries = 0\n      while diff != 0 and tries < 1000:\n          tries += 1\n          if diff > 0:\n              idx = int(rng.integers(0, L))\n              if counts[idx] < high:\n                  counts[idx] += 1\n                  diff -= 1\n          else:\n              idx = int(rng.integers(0, L))\n              if counts[idx] > low:\n                  counts[idx] -= 1\n                  diff += 1\n      return counts if sum(counts) == target and all(low <= k <= high for k in counts) else None\n\n  def _sample_patterns(samples: int = 120, rng=None):\n\
    \      if rng is None:\n          rng = np.random.default_rng(123)\n      patterns = set()\n      while len(patterns) < samples:\n          L = int(rng.integers(6, 8))  # 6 or 7 rows\n          p = _make_pattern(L, target=32, rng=rng)\n          if p is not None:\n              patterns.add(tuple(p))\n      return [list(p) for p in patterns]\n\n  # ---------------- Local search primitives ----------------\n\n  def _armijo_step(C, G, alpha0):\n      nrm = np.linalg.norm(G, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      Ddir = G / np.maximum(nrm, 1e-12)\n      C_best = C\n      r_best, obj_best, ok, *_ = _solve_lp(C_best)\n      if not ok:\n          return C, r_best, obj_best\n      alpha = alpha0\n      for _ in range(8):\n          C_try = _clip_box(C + alpha * Ddir, margin=1e-6)\n          r_try, obj_try, ok, *_ = _solve_lp(C_try)\n          if ok and obj_try > obj_best + 1e-12:\n              C_best, r_best, obj_best = C_try, r_try, obj_try\n              alpha *= 1.25\n          else:\n         \
    \     alpha *= 0.5\n      return C_best, r_best, obj_best\n\n  def _micro_jitter(C0: np.ndarray, steps: int = 120, sigma: float = 0.007, seed: int = 2029):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      C_best = C0.copy()\n      r_best, obj_best, ok, *_ = _solve_lp(C_best)\n      if not ok:\n          return C0, r_best, obj_best\n      for _ in range(steps):\n          C_try = C_best.copy()\n          i = int(rng.integers(0, C_try.shape[0]))\n          d = rng.normal(0.0, sigma, size=2)\n          C_try[i] = _clip_box(C_try[i] + d)\n          r_try, obj_try, ok, *_ = _solve_lp(C_try)\n          if ok and obj_try > obj_best + 1e-12:\n              C_best, r_best, obj_best = C_try, r_try, obj_try\n      return C_best, r_best, obj_best\n\n  def _worst_circle_relocate(C: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray, attempts: int = 60, margin: float = 0.02, seed: int = 2030):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      idx = int(np.argmin(r))\n      C_best = C.copy()\n      r_best, obj_best, ok, *_ =\
    \ _solve_lp(C_best)\n      if not ok:\n          return C, r, obj_best\n      for _ in range(attempts):\n          cand = rng.uniform(margin, 1.0 - margin, size=2)\n          C_try = C.copy()\n          C_try[idx] = cand\n          C_try = _clip_box(C_try)\n          r_try, obj_try, ok, *_ = _solve_lp(C_try)\n          if ok and obj_try > obj_best + 1e-12:\n              C_best, r_best, obj_best = C_try, r_try, obj_try\n      return C_best, r_best, obj_best\n\n  def _dual_gradient_optimize(C_init: np.ndarray, time_budget: float = 140.0, seed: int = 0):\n      t0 = time.time()\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      C = C_init.copy()\n      r, obj, ok, y_pairs, u_upper, I, J = _solve_lp(C)\n      if not ok:\n          return C_init, r, obj\n      best_C, best_r, best_obj = C.copy(), r.copy(), obj\n      alpha = 0.03\n      it = 0\n\n      while time.time() - t0 < time_budget:\n          G = _grad_from_duals(C, y_pairs, I, J) + _grad_boundary(C, r, u_upper, weight=0.12)\n     \
    \     C_new, r_new, obj_new = _armijo_step(C, G, alpha)\n          if obj_new > obj + 1e-12:\n              C, r, obj = C_new, r_new, obj_new\n              if obj > best_obj + 1e-12:\n                  best_C, best_r, best_obj = C.copy(), r.copy(), obj\n              alpha = min(0.08, alpha * 1.12)\n          else:\n              alpha = max(0.004, alpha * 0.6)\n              C, r, obj = _micro_jitter(C, steps=40, sigma=0.006, seed=int(rng.integers(1, 10_000_000)))\n              r, obj, ok, y_pairs, u_upper, I, J = _solve_lp(C)\n              if not ok:\n                  break\n              if obj > best_obj + 1e-12:\n                  best_C, best_r, best_obj = C.copy(), r.copy(), obj\n\n          it += 1\n          if it % 6 == 0 and (time.time() - t0) < time_budget - 2.0:\n              C2, r2, obj2 = _worst_circle_relocate(C, r, attempts=50, margin=0.02, seed=int(rng.integers(1, 10_000_000)))\n              if obj2 > obj + 1e-12:\n                  C, r, obj = C2, r2, obj2\n\
    \                  if obj > best_obj + 1e-12:\n                      best_C, best_r, best_obj = C.copy(), r.copy(), obj\n              r, obj, ok, y_pairs, u_upper, I, J = _solve_lp(C)\n              if not ok:\n                  break\n\n      return best_C, best_r, best_obj\n\n  # ---------------- Persistence ----------------\n\n  SAVE_PATH = 'storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz'\n\n  def _load_persisted():\n      if os.path.exists(SAVE_PATH):\n          try:\n              data = np.load(SAVE_PATH)\n              C = data['centers'].astype(float)\n              r = data['radii'].astype(float)\n              if C.shape == (32, 2) and r.shape == (32,):\n                  return C, r, float(np.sum(r))\n          except Exception:\n              return None\n      return None\n\n  def _save_best(C, r):\n      try:\n          os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(SAVE_PATH), exist_ok=True)\n          np.savez(SAVE_PATH, centers=C, radii=r, obj=float(np.sum(r)))\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n\
    \n  # ---------------- Main entry ----------------\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n\n      # Strong baseline\n      C_base, r_base, obj_base = _baseline_grid_6x6_minus_corners()\n      best_C, best_r, best_obj = C_base, r_base, obj_base\n\n      candidates = []\n\n      # Include persisted best if present\n      persisted = _load_persisted()\n      if persisted is not None:\n          Cp, rp, objp = persisted\n          rp2, objp2, ok, *_ = _solve_lp(Cp)\n          if ok:\n              rp2 = _safety_shrink(Cp, rp2)\n              candidates.append((float(np.sum(rp2)), Cp))\n\n      # Generate many random tri/hex patterns and parameterizations\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(271828)\n      patterns = _sample_patterns(samples=140, rng=rng)\n      for counts in patterns:\n          y_margin = float(rng.uniform(0.065, 0.12))\n          fudge_x = float(rng.uniform(0.92, 1.05))\n          C = _tri_hex_layout(counts, y_margin=y_margin, x_margin=y_margin, fudge_x=fudge_x)\n\
    \          if C.shape != (32, 2):\n              continue\n          r, obj, ok, *_ = _solve_lp(C)\n          if ok:\n              candidates.append((obj, C))\n\n      # Add farthest-sampling seed and baseline\n      C_far = _farthest_sampling(n=32, margin=0.02, candidates=600, seed=2048)\n      r_far, obj_far, ok, *_ = _solve_lp(C_far)\n      if ok:\n          candidates.append((obj_far, C_far))\n      candidates.append((obj_base, C_base))\n\n      # Rank and keep top unique starts\n      cand_sorted = sorted(candidates, key=lambda t: t[0], reverse=True)\n      unique = []\n      seen = set()\n      for obj, C in cand_sorted:\n          key = tuple(np.round(C.flatten(), 4))\n          if key not in seen:\n              unique.append((obj, C))\n              seen.add(key)\n          if len(unique) >= 6:\n              break\n\n      # Refine top candidates under a safe total runtime\n      budgets = [150.0, 110.0, 80.0, 60.0, 45.0, 35.0]  # <= 480s worst-case\n      for idx, (obj0,\
    \ C0) in enumerate(unique):\n          Cb, Rb, Ob = _dual_gradient_optimize(C0, time_budget=budgets[idx], seed=3000 + idx)\n          if Ob > best_obj + 1e-12:\n              best_C, best_r, best_obj = Cb, Rb, Ob\n\n      # Finalize with safety shrink and validation; ensure non-regression\n      best_r = _safety_shrink(best_C, best_r, factor=0.9995)\n      if not _validate(best_C, best_r) or best_obj < obj_base - 1e-9:\n          return C_base, r_base\n\n      _save_best(best_C, best_r)\n      return best_C, best_r\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating research approach**\n\nI'm assessing our next steps in Research Counter. We’ve made some improvements, starting with a baseline of 2.666, then hitting 2.7972 with alternating triangular rows. The latest method, Tri-hex multi-start, is still running, and we're yet to score it. Now, I need to refine our approach to maximize the sum of radii for 32 circles. This involves optimizing center positions while ensuring non-overlapping arrangements. The goal is tricky since it's not typical to maximize these sums.**Proposing optimization strategies**\n\nThis problem is quite complex, but we can propose a gradient approach using a differentiable surrogate of LP optimum with dual variables. However, we might find better deterministic constructions. Historically, the optimum sum of radii in the unit square equals 1 when radii are variable. But for 32 fixed circles, we need to think creatively! We could pack larger circles along the edges while using tiny circles elsewhere.\
  \ The maximum radius is limited to 0.5. So maybe one circle could be 0.5, with the rest being very small.**Considering circle placement**\n\nI need to ensure that the small circles don’t overlap with the large circle, meaning they have to be radially outside that big circle and within the boundary. The large circle, with a radius of 0.5, touches the square's edges when centered at (0.5, 0.5). However, there are triangular corner regions outside this large circle where I can fit small circles. Their radii can reach about the distance to the boundary, but they still mustn’t overlap with the big circle. I'll need to calculate their placement carefully!**Determining small circle radius**\n\nSince the radius of the big circle is 0.5, any small circle centered at a distance \\(d\\) from (0.5, 0.5) can have a radius \\(r_{small} \\leq d - 0.5\\). This limits us to a maximum \\(r_{small}\\). For a corner like (0,0), \\(d = 0.7071\\), which means \\(r_{small} \\leq 0.2071\\). We also need to factor\
  \ in that the small circle should touch both edges and the big circle. I can use the classic Apollonian method to find the optimal radius!**Exploring circle tangency conditions**\n\nTo maintain tangency between the small circle and both edges and the big circle with radius 0.5, we must meet specific conditions. I've found that calculating the distance and setting inequalities shows this isn’t possible for circles of certain sizes. Tangential arrangements would imply the small circle can be up to about 0.08578 without overlap. \n\nGroups of smaller circles can be positioned at the corners, and if I optimize their radius, I might achieve a better sum than initially estimated. Exploring advanced optimization algorithms seems like a good next step!**Optimizing circle packing**\n\nI’m examining the constraints for fixed and variable centers in the optimization problem. The challenges stem from pairwise distance constraints, but I see that these norms might not yield a convex set, complicating\
  \ global optimization.\n\nIt might be better to explore heuristics to push the sum of radii further. I’ve considered various configurations for rows of circles and optimized through iterative techniques. There's potential for improvement if I adjust the layout or parameterize the arrangement more finely. Perhaps I should also devise a smoother penalty approach to help ensure constraint satisfaction while optimizing.**Refining circle packing optimization**\n\nI’m considering improvements for the optimization problem, specifically for adjusting center positions and pairwise constraints. Adding second-order heuristics might help improve slack in the system. \n\nAlso, exploring equal radii could minimize fairness, but variable sizes may yield a larger sum. I’m inspired to refine triangular arrangements and y-positions while implementing code to surpass our current best result of 2.7972. I think a coarse-to-fine parameter search might be efficient, alongside micro-jitter methods for optimal\
  \ arrangements. There's potential here, and I’m eager to try these ideas.**Developing a combinatorial pattern search**\n\nI'm thinking about generating new patterns with a focus on arranging 5 and 6 row counts. Adjusting x and y separations is key to fitting everything neatly. I'll implement a new function to create patterns summing to 32, ensuring two consecutive rows aren’t both 6 while sampling combinations to optimize configurations.\n\nI need to manage runtime effectively while maximizing the arrangement. We’ll also include adjustments to avoid overlap and maintain feasible center placements, aiming for more efficient patterns with a \"dual gradient refine.\" It’s all about smart iteration and systematic exploration!**Implementing efficient code structure**\n\nI'm working on crafting a robust code setup. This involves defining functions for pairwise indices, boundary margins, and solving linear programming problems to obtain duals. I want to ensure I evaluate candidates quickly and\
  \ retain the best options. \n\nI'll be including persistence paths to load and save configurations while being mindful of runtime budgets, aiming for quick evaluations. I also need to design flexible pattern generation methods for various row counts and ensure uniqueness across samples. It's important to maintain clear documentation as I structure everything!**Refining optimization code**\n\nI'm focusing on refining the top candidates through dual gradient optimization with defined budgets. I’ll add micro-jitter functionality and incorporate worst-circle relocation methods to enhance performance. The code block is likely to grow, but I can manage it. \n\nI’ll finalize the YAML file for submission, ensuring the title is correctly formatted and includes necessary quotes around potentially problematic characters. After that, I’ll proceed with the submission of the entire script, aiming to make sure everything is in order for a smooth finish!"
