parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto reflect}\n=/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Goal: Design and scope a practical active-set Newton solver for joint centers+radii to push beyond the current 2.9292366 sum-of-radii score, while keeping runtime and robustness within the station’s limits.\n\n  Constraints:\n  - Variables: centers C ∈ R^{32×2}, radii r ∈ R^{32}, objective maximize 1ᵀr.\n  - Feasibility: boundary r_i ≤ x_i, r_i ≤ 1−x_i, r_i ≤ y_i, r_i ≤ 1−y_i; non-overlap r_i + r_j ≤ ||c_i − c_j||.\n  - Current strengths: \n    * LP (HiGHS) gives exact radii for fixed centers (and duals).\n    * Dual-aware steps (block-Newton) and MM-LP trust-region work well.\n    * Clearance relocations + per-circle line search escape local traps.\n  - Weaknesses:\n    * Pure trust-constr underperforms here (stability/precision).\n    * SOCP alone plateaus unless warm-started by good heuristics.\n\n  Design questions:\n  1) Active-set selection:\n     - Use LP duals (pairwise y_ij ≥ 0 and boundary\
    \ u_i ≥ 0) to identify near-active constraints: \n       A_pair = {(i,j): y_ij ≥ τ_pair} and A_bound = {i,k ∈ {left,right,down,up}: u_i^k ≥ τ_b}.\n     - Keep a margin-set M with soft-thresholds for potential activation.\n     - How to tune τ thresholds and hysteresis to prevent thrashing?\n\n  2) Newton/KKT system (centers-only step):\n     - Treat radii as dependent via LP; differentiate KKT conditions implicitly is heavy. Practical alternative:\n       * Use current y_ij,u_i as Lagrange multipliers and form a quadratic model in δC that increases distances for A_pair and boundary margins for A_bound.\n       * Objective proxy: maximize ∑ r_i ≈ linear gain predicted by duals: Δ ≈ ∑_{(i,j)∈A_pair} y_ij ⟨u_ij, δc_i − δc_j⟩ + ∑_{i∈A_bound} u_i ⟨v_i, δc_i⟩ \n         where u_ij is unit vector along (c_i−c_j) and v_i points away from nearest boundary face.\n       * Curvature: H ≈ ∑_{(i,j)∈A_pair} (y_ij / d_ij) (I − u_ij u_ijᵀ) assembled into 2×2 blocks per circle (as in block-Newton), plus\
    \ small damping λI.\n       * Solve (H + λI) δC = g, where g stacks per-circle gradients from duals and boundary.\n\n  3) Trust region and acceptance:\n     - Compute trial C' = clip(C + α δC), with α from backtracking.\n     - Re-solve exact LP for r' and accept if ∑r' improves > ε and feasibility holds.\n     - Adapt λ, α, and active-set thresholds based on agreement ratio (predicted vs actual gain).\n\n  4) Active-set updates:\n     - If many constraints violate after step, enlarge active set; if all slack grows, relax set.\n     - Periodically refresh by re-solving LP to obtain new duals; keep top-k pairs by y_ij and top boundary faces by u_i.\n\n  5) Integration with current pipeline:\n     - Insert “Active-Set Newton” (ASN) between MM-LP and block-Newton:\n       Seeds → MM-LP (few iters) → ASN (5–10 iterations) → block-Newton (polish) → optional SOCP polish → exact LP finalize → strict shrink → persist if improved.\n     - Runtime budgeting: ASN should use small linear solves\
    \ (64×64 for 32 circles × 2D) with Cholesky; cheap compared to LP solves.\n\n  6) Robustness:\n     - Keep deterministic RNG and iteration caps.\n     - If ASN stalls (no gain in 3 consecutive accepted steps), revert to clearance relocations or per-circle line search.\n     - Always finalize with exact LP + strict shrink and require strict feasibility.\n\n  Deliverables for next coding turn:\n  - Implement ASN module:\n    * Active-set builder from duals with thresholds and hysteresis.\n    * Gradient g and block-diagonal H assembly (with optional sparse neighbor coupling if cheap).\n    * Trust-region line search and acceptance against exact LP.\n  - Integrate into best-performing multi-start script and guard with persistence discipline.\ntick: 5\n```"
