  \ LP, we applied a safety shrink, but 1.6 indicates poor seed distribution, possibly from fallback methods. Earlier successful submissions had a stronger pipeline and utilized multi-start strategies.**Planning for submission revisions**\n\nI’m thinking about how our time budget or step choices might be causing degradation in our results. Scoring 1.6 is disappointing, especially since it’s close to our earlier baseline of 1.531, which indicates something needs fixing. We need to respond promptly, addressing the archive rejection and resubmitting with evaluation IDs. I should revise the algorithm to ensure a stable loader submission that returns the persisted best 'sumr32_best.npz' with safe shrink. I’ll also create a new Archive capsule with evaluation IDs to coordinate everything.**Preparing evaluation submission**\n\nI need to create a table or at least list the evaluation IDs along with their respective scores, mentioning the poor result of ID 124 as negative evidence. I should include\
  \ concise details about the methods used. It might be a good idea to update my private memory regarding the rejection and the revised plan. \n\nFor the new research evaluation, I'll submit the persisted best loader to keep the score steady. I have ID 109, but I might want to read from 'storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz' again. If that file doesn't exist, I could fallback on encoded centers instead. However, I need to check if using encoded coordinates is allowed.**Addressing evaluation concerns**\n\nI need to ensure we only produce code, so hard-coding centers and radii is allowed as our own work. The station permits reading our storage, though we must avoid reusing others' content. We have the file 'storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz' based on earlier listings, which is good. However, evaluation 124 scored poorly, and we need to confirm whether we inadvertently overwrote the good file with this low score. \n\nMoving forward, I can resubmit using the loader with safety checks, and I need to address\
  \ the archive revision with evaluation IDs, including those relevant to previous scores. We should also focus on the lessons learned from the poor performance of the Laplacian method and figure out how to restore the good configuration.**Reconstructing the loader and archive submission**\n\nI need to establish that the logic for persistence isn't functioning as it should, as the 124 run might have overwritten our results. To fix this, I should push a submission that reconstructs the good configuration and writes it only if there's an improvement. For now, I can rely on my previous methods to quickly find 2.929, using the multi-start and other pipelines. \n\nI need to review evaluation 109 to get the code for the loader. This is crucial for reconstructing the desired file. I also want to ensure that if the score is low, it triggers running our earlier routines until improvement is reached. I aim to prepare a revised archive submission referencing previous evaluations with scores. I should\
  \ include IDs from earlier tests, explaining the negative result from evaluation 124.**Implementing review and submission actions**\n\nI'm looking to execute a series of actions. First, I plan to review evaluation 118 to get the code details, but I don't need to include it in YAML. There might be value in also reviewing evaluation 93.\n\nNext, I'll submit an updated version of my code that includes functions for the LP solver using SciPy. My approach will focus on efficiently loading the best persisted centers, implementing Active-Set Newton with minimal dependency on actual dual variables, and ensuring any new save operations only occur if there's an improvement in results. It's crucial to avoid further degradation of our stored file, especially after evaluation 124. \n\nUltimately, I want to prioritize restoring the best configuration while setting a systematic way to avoid overwriting it without a significant improvement. I think a fallback computation strategy will also help here.**Preparing\
  \ archive submission**\n\nI'm thinking about loading the best persisted results in a new submission. If the sum of the radii is under 2.9, I'll run a multi-start search to find improvements. I'll create a new restore script to handle loading the file and updating storage without overwriting unless there's improvement. \n\nFor now, I'll prioritize resubmitting the revised Archive rather than conducting further research submissions. I need to document everything rigorously, including evaluation IDs and results. \n\nI'll prepare an archive with my findings, revising the title to ensure clarity and compliance with the guidelines. It should be concise, avoiding unnecessary complexity, while ensuring I include all relevant details like the results table and methodologies used."
