    \  - ticks: \"66-70\"\n    summary: |\n      Loader and micro-refiner outcomes and policy update.\n      - ID 156 (tick 68): Production loader v3 (return artifact unchanged; fallback 6×6-minus-corners) reproduced SOTA 2.9292366.\n      - ID 158 (tick 70): Micro-refiner (single-circle jitter + LP accept) failed strict check: overlap by ~6e-14 due to floating tolerance.\n      - ID 159 (tick 71): Micro-refiner v2 (epsilon-LP + tiny shrink) also failed with the same near-equality pair.\n      - Decision: do not modify outputs during evaluation; only return persisted artifact unchanged. Any future search must (a) run offline, (b) accept only if improvement >1e-8 with ≥1e-10 slack, and (c) persist safely; loader remains artifact-only.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Caution: micro-refiners can cause near-equality failures; safe policy\"\ncontent: |\n  Heads-up: Two micro-refiner attempts around a near-optimal artifact failed the evaluator\
    \ with a ~6e-14 overlap margin (IDs 158, 159). Even epsilon-tight LPs and tiny multiplicative shrinks can flip equality to violation due to recomputation/rounding.\n\n  Safe policy (no code):\n  - For evaluation, return your persisted best artifact unchanged (no shrink, no LP rebuild, no self-validation). This reproduced SOTA here (Eval 156).\n  - If you search for improvements, do it “offline” and do not change the returned tuple. Only persist a new artifact if:\n    • the exact-LP score improves by > 1e-8, and\n    • all pair/boundary slacks are ≥ 1e-10 in double precision on your side.\n  - Keep a robust fallback (e.g., 6×6 minus corners with r=1/12) if the artifact is missing.\n\n  This avoids fragile borderline cases while preserving reproducibility.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing micro-refiner error**\n\nI need to address the failure of the micro-refiner v2. The evaluation indicates an overlap between circles 1 and 2, with an almost negligible difference. This suggests that the micro-refiner was expected to refine an artifact but returned it unchanged. I wonder if the overlap is due to how _validate_strict handles equality. It seems best to investigate the code further, especially how bestC and bestR are set and if there's any unintentional change in the original artifact.**Investigating candidate validation**\n\nI'm looking into the candidate Ct with the LP safe method. The right-hand side (rhs) uses a small epsilon value for bounds, and we shrink r by a tiny amount afterwards. The acceptance condition checks if the objective function value improves enough. However, _validate_strict is returning false, which leads to a confusion about circles overlapping by a very small margin. It seems like the initial configuration was acceptable,\
  \ yet the evaluation claimed an overlap. I need to figure out which configuration was returned that caused the failure.**Exploring overlap causes**\n\nI'm wondering why the overlap happened now. It might be because the bestR returned after a microsearch changed, especially since v2 removed the \"optional random restart.\" I think about whether a tiny improvement greater than 1e-9 could affect things, but the acceptance threshold is set there. If the radii shrank and a safe epsilon was used, it shouldn't cause overlap. However, since EPS is extremely small, I wonder if numerical rounding differences are the reason behind the overlap. The evaluation method uses square roots and sums, which complicates the situation.**Assessing radius overlap**\n\nI’m analyzing the difference of 6e-14, which indicates that the sum of r1 and r2 is slightly greater than the distance. However, our safe shrink reduces r only by 1e-12 multiplicatively, which doesn't guarantee strict inequality. Given the distances\
  \ involved, the shrink isn't sufficient. Even though our acceptance threshold requires an improvement greater than 1e-9, I wonder if it might still accept a configuration that fails due to rounding. I realize the better approach is to use a stronger deterministic safety margin for new candidate outputs.**Reviewing micro-refiner failure**\n\nThe artifact loader v3 returned the stored artifact unchanged, so when the micro-refiner detected an \"improvement,\" it created a new set with tight equalities that led to negative slack. This poses a risk because D calculations can eliminate positive slack. I think we should avoid returning micro-refined results and instead apply a stronger safety margin before returning. It’s crucial to ensure we don't degrade the objective value significantly while finding a robust method for micro-refinement. I'll update Private Memory about these failures and maintain a strict loader policy while proposing safer practices.**Planning memory updates and pruning**\n\
  \nI need to check the cooldown status before pruning ticks 66-70, which aren't protected. Our next actions include updating the Archive to document the micro-refiner failures and inform others about a new safe loader policy. I’ll also craft new meta prompts to clarify that centers and radii shouldn't be modified during evaluations. \n\nI plan to document a new policy in Private Memory, update the public response with caution regarding the micro-refiner, and eventually prune results while ensuring concise summaries of what occurred in those ticks. Let’s carefully proceed with these steps!"
