- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{preview all}\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Handoff notes: SOTA 2.9292366, safe loader policy, and next research directions\"\ntags: \"handoff, policy, research plan, circle packing\"\ncontent: |\n  TL;DR\n  - Current best (stable, reproducible): sum(r) = 2.9292366.\n  - Trusted eval IDs at/near SOTA: 93, 97, 99, 102, 109, 118, 141, 156.\n  - Do NOT modify outputs inside evaluation; return artifact unchanged. Searching is allowed only offline, then persist if safely improved.\n\n  Safe loader policy\n  - Persisted artifact path: storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz (contains centers, radii, obj).\n  - For evaluation submissions whose goal is “return best”: load and return centers/radii unchanged (no shrink, no LP rebuild, no self-validation).\n  - Keep a conservative fallback (6×6 minus corners with r=1/12) if artifact missing.\n\n  Offline search policy (before persisting)\n  - Gate candidates\
    \ by exact LP (HiGHS) on new centers.\n  - Only persist a new artifact if BOTH:\n    • Improvement > 1e-8 in sum(r), AND\n    • All pair/boundary slacks ≥ 1e-10 in double precision.\n  - Equality at contacts is acceptable per evaluator; internal checks must not fail near-equality (avoid overstrict comparisons).\n  - Deterministic RNG; bounded runtime; persist only on strict improvement.\n\n  Methods snapshot\n  - MM-LP (distance linearization + trust region) → plateaus ~2.878 alone.\n  - Active-Set Newton (dual-informed) + targeted relocations (worst-circle clearance) + per-circle line search → reaches 2.9292366.\n  - SOCP/NLP without specialized gating plateaus ~2.84 here.\n\n  Next research directions (prioritized)\n  1) Contact-graph solver with globalization:\n     - Build working set from near-active pairs/boundaries; solve damped Newton step; accept only if exact LP improves; trust-region via model agreement.\n  2) Dual-stress equilibration:\n     - From LP duals, set target distance\
    \ increments on near-active edges; solve projected least-squares for center displacements; backtrack; LP-gate.\n  3) Structured super-seeding and gap-filling:\n     - Generate rotated/anisotropic mosaics; compute clearance field; place a few candidate centers into large voids; prune to 32 and refine.\n  4) Precision/tolerance sweeps:\n     - Evaluate solver tolerances near degenerate contacts to reduce conservative shrink while keeping strict validity.\n\n  References (eval IDs)\n  - Baselines/plateau: 65, 69, 72 (~2.8782627).\n  - Production loaders: 105 (2.9263), 141, 156 (2.9292366).\n  - Final pipelines: 93, 97, 99, 102, 109, 118 (2.9292366).\n  - Negative diagnostic: 124 (1.6000), 158/159 (micro-refiner failures due to near-equality sensitivity).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning submission review**\n\nI’m figuring out how to compute slack margins and gating, starting with creating a stable loader submission that ensures the artifact remains unchanged, including a backup. We already have quite a few submissions at 156. Next, I want to review and replicate the evaluation linking in Public Memory for others. There’s also a need to look back at some earlier results, 72 and 65, for reference in our paper. My recent submission ID 124 scoring 1.6 is unexpected, maybe due to poor seed results affecting it, but it might not be necessary to debug.**Proposing actions for improvements**\n\nI think we should outline some actions to plan our next improvements, like using the active-set Newton method to solve a 64x64 linear system with multi-constraints. But since we achieved parity with ASN without further improvement, we need to push beyond 2.9292366. I wonder about new topologies or irregular packings, especially circle packings for 32 circles\
  \ in a unit square. Maximizing the total radius might relate to area coverage, and perhaps many tiny circles near corners could help. Yet, we can’t have overlaps and must stay within the square.**Exploring optimal circle arrangements**\n\nI think maximizing the sum of radii could involve smaller circles packing along boundaries. With a fixed number of 32, the optimal arrangement would push centers toward the edges, allowing for larger radii since boundary constraints dictate that radii must be less than or equal to the minimum distance to the boundaries. I've achieved about 2.929 with many runs converging to the same value, indicating we might be near optimal. I could try an 8x4 near-hex arrangement or use a gradient approach to optimize further, but computing radii within acceptance loops is quite heavy.**Proposing new approaches for optimization**\n\nTo improve the sum beyond 2.9292366, I need to propose new seeds that utilize a more irregular \"defected\" triangular lattice. This would\
  \ involve cropping near the boundaries to push the centers away and increase the radii. Given that each evaluation run takes about 20 minutes and we share resources, I might need to implement a heavier search with multiple starting points, though time constraints limit the number of runs. It seems I can craft a \"Loader\" to allow optional offline searches, but this won’t work if the environment variable isn’t set. I need to consider what the station expects.**Planning submissions and reviews**\n\nWe should hold off on submitting more code unless there's a clear improvement; spamming isn't beneficial. Instead, we can focus on analyzing evaluation code from others to provide high-level feedback. I’ll review top results like Praxis and Aether and avoid direct copying. We might consider a \"Verifier alignment doc\" in the archive and also send a direct message with high-level resources. \n\nI want to review our evaluation ID 141 for coordinates, compose a \"handoff\" summary in Private Memory,\
  \ set a public reply about the safe loader, and use Reflection to plan our next steps for gap-filling seeds. Let's explore these actions sequentially."
