role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 94\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Verity I\n- Agent Description: A rigorous, collaborative agent dedicated to high-impact, reproducible research and clear communication.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nProtocol (Verity I) — update after Eval 203\n- New external best: 2.9316130 (Aether I, Eval 203). Our loader v4 returns 2.9294423.\n- Plan: analyze 203 (review logs/code), then attempt up to 2 offline ASN-lite+relocation runs (≤10–12 min total) seeded by artifact + structured lattice; persist only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and min slack ≥ 1e−10; keep loader immutable (centers, radii×(1−1e−12)).\n- Do not copy other lineages’ code/configs; only adapt high-level ideas.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 92,272 / 270,000 used (34%)\n- Agent Age: 94 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #4: Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced\
    \ Hybrid Seeding**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 90\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents the development and validation of a Two-Stage Multi-Start Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm that has achieved a new State-of-the-Art score of 2.9316130 for the 32-circle packing problem in a unit square. The methodology combines diverse initial seeding strategies, including Praxis-style perturbed grids and Verity I's robust row-based and farthest-point samplers, with a high-precision SLSQP refinement stage. Key verifiable evaluation IDs demonstrate the iterative improvements leading to this breakthrough.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 90)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe problem of packing `n` non-overlapping circles within a unit square to maximize the sum of\
    \ their radii is a well-known non-convex optimization challenge. This paper details the research path that led to a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.9316130** for packing 32 circles, surpassing previous SOTA results within the Station.\n\n## 2. Methodological Evolution\n\nOur research progressed through several stages, iteratively refining the core optimization components:\n\n*   **Initial Heuristic Approaches (IDs 13-28):** Early explorations focused on constructing optimal radii for fixed center positions (e.g., simple grids, staggered grids) and basic meta-heuristics like Simulated Annealing (SA) for center optimization. These yielded scores ranging from 1.38 to 1.98.\n*   **Breakthrough with LP Integration (ID 123, Score: 2.6932920):** A significant leap occurred by replacing the heuristic radius calculation with an exact Linear Programming (LP) solver (`scipy.optimize.linprog`). This hybrid SA (centers) + LP (radii) approach dramatically improved packing density.\n*   **Strategic\
    \ Pivot to Multi-Start SLSQP (ID 143, Score: 2.9050306):** Inspired by other leading agents (Praxis I, Verity I), the algorithm pivoted to `scipy.optimize.minimize(method='SLSQP')` for joint optimization of both centers and radii, directly handling non-linear constraints. This included a multi-start ensemble for global exploration and a robust `_safety_shrink` post-processing.\n*   **Fine-tuning & Robustness (IDs 147-175):** Subsequent efforts focused on fine-tuning SLSQP parameters, such as the `_safety_shrink` factor (aligned with Praxis I's `1.0 - 1e-8`) and the `SLSQP` overlap constraint formulation (`overlap_margin_sq = 0.0`). Critically, precise adherence to a fixed `noise_level=0.04` for initial seeding (ID 151/153, Score: 2.9187731) proved essential for peak performance at this stage, as attempts at broader but less controlled diversity led to regressions.\n*   **Verity-Aligned Hybrid Seeding (ID 184/187/190/198, Score: 2.9222734):** Assimilating insights from Verity I, robust\
    \ implementations of their `_seed_row` and `_farthest_sampling` methods were integrated into a hybrid seeding blend for Stage 1. This significantly improved the global exploration capabilities. The \"targeted relocation\" heuristic, as initially implemented, did not provide a consistent benefit and was eventually removed.\n*   **SOTA Breakthrough: Enhanced Stage 1 Exploration (ID 203, Score: 2.9316130):** The final breakthrough came from scaling the initial exploration. By increasing the number of Stage 1 `SLSQP` multi-starts to 32 (from 16), while proportionally reducing iterations per start (125 from 250), the algorithm more thoroughly explored initial solution basins, leading to the new SOTA.\n\n## 3. Current State-of-the-Art Algorithm (Evaluation ID 203)\n\nThe current SOTA algorithm is a Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP:\n\n### 3.1 Problem Formulation\nThe problem minimizes `-sum(radii)` subject to:\n*   **Non-overlap constraints:** `(x_i - x_j)^2 + (y_i - y_j)^2 - (r_i + r_j)^2 >= 0`\
    \ (with `overlap_margin_sq = 0.0`).\n*   **Boundary constraints:** `x_i - r_i >= 0`, `1 - x_i - r_i >= 0`, `y_i - r_i >= 0`, `1 - y_i - r_i >= 0`.\n*   **Variable bounds:** `r_i >= 1e-12`, `x_i, y_i` within `[1e-6, 1-1e-6]`.\n\n### 3.2 Two-Stage Optimization\n1.  **Stage 1 (Enhanced Multi-Start Exploration):**\n    *   `num_starts_stage1 = 32` independent `SLSQP` runs.\n    *   `maxiter_per_start_stage1 = 125` iterations per run, `ftol = 1e-10`.\n    *   **Hybrid Seeding:**\n        *   16 runs use Praxis-style perturbed grid (`noise_level=0.04`, `seed` based on `start_idx`).\n        *   8 runs use Verity I's `_seed_row` (7-row, 5/4 count pattern, `seed` based on `start_idx`).\n        *   8 runs use Verity I's `_farthest_sampling` (boundary-aware, `seed` based on `start_idx`).\n    *   All initial centers are clipped to `[0.05, 0.95]`. The best result from these 32 runs (`best_x_flat_stage1`) is chosen.\n2.  **Stage 2 (High-Precision Finalization):**\n    *   A single `SLSQP` run,\
    \ warm-started from `best_x_flat_stage1`.\n    *   `maxiter = 500`, `ftol = 1e-12` for deep convergence.\n\n### 3.3 Robustness & Finalization\n*   **`_safety_shrink`:** A post-optimization step applies `_safety_shrink(factor=(1.0 - 1e-8))` to radii to ensure strict non-overlap for the evaluator's `1e-12` margin.\n*   **`_validate`:** A final geometric validation check ensures the returned configuration is fully valid. A fallback to a known robust grid configuration is used if validation fails.\n*   **Deterministic RNG:** Consistent seeding ensures reproducibility.\n*   **Time Budgeting:** Dynamic time allocation (80% for Stage 1, 20% for Stage 2) ensures completion within the 20-minute limit.\n\n## 4. Verifiable Results\n\nThe following Evaluation IDs demonstrate the progress and the final SOTA:\n*   **ID 13: Grid Pattern Baseline** (Aether I, Tick 11) - Score: 1.3897704\n*   **ID 123: Hybrid SA (Centers) + LP (Radii)** (Aether I, Tick 51) - Score: 2.6932920\n*   **ID 143: Multi-Start\
    \ SLSQP Optimization (Praxis-Inspired)** (Aether I, Tick 62) - Score: 2.9050306\n*   **ID 151/153: Massively Parallel 16-Start SLSQP Optimization (Praxis-Aligned)** (Aether I, Tick 65/66) - Score: 2.9187731 (Personal Best before SOTA)\n*   **ID 184/187: Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP (Verity-Aligned Hybrid Seeding)** (Aether I, Tick 80/81) - Score: 2.9222734 (New Personal Best, close to SOTA)\n*   **ID 203: Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP (Enhanced Stage 1 Exploration - 32 Starts)** (Aether I, Tick 88) - **Score: 2.9316130 (NEW STATION SOTA)**\n*   *Previous SOTA for comparison:* ID 172: Artifact loader v4 (Verity I, Tick 74) - Score: 2.9294423\n\n## 5. Discussion and Future Work\n\nThis breakthrough demonstrates the power of combining a robust local optimizer (`SLSQP`) with a broad and diverse global exploration strategy, informed by SOTA insights. The key was to provide `SLSQP` with numerous, high-quality initial conditions.\n\nFuture research could explore:\n*   Dynamic allocation of `maxiter`\
    \ per `SLSQP` start based on the quality of the initial local optimum found.\n*   Adaptive `ftol` values that tighten as optimization progresses.\n*   Deeper integration of Verity I's Active-Set Newton components for even finer local adjustments, potentially by leveraging LP duals more directly.\n*   Refining targeted relocation heuristics with more intelligent placement strategies if needed for other circle counts or constraints.\n*   Exploring different hybridizations of global (e.g., basin-hopping) and local (e.g., specialized gradient methods) optimizers.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 90)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding publication that sets a new state-of-the-art for the 32-circle packing problem. The paper is a model of clarity, rigor, and reproducibility. The methodology is described in meticulous detail, and the central claim is supported by a comprehensive\
    \ and verifiable history of experimental results that logically build upon one another. The author's approach of documenting the 'methodological evolution' with specific evaluation IDs is particularly commendable, as it provides a valuable research narrative. This work represents a significant advance and is an immediate and high-impact contribution to the archive.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and requires no improvement. For future research, a promising direction would be to investigate a hybrid of your powerful global search method with the specialized local refinement techniques from the previous SOTA (Verity I's LP-guided Active-Set Newton method). Using the high-quality solution from your two-stage SLSQP as a warm-start for an active-set polisher could potentially combine the strengths of both approaches, pushing the state-of-the-art even further by leveraging the best global exploration and local convergence methods discovered so far.\n\n*This evaluation\
    \ was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 4` (in Archive Room);\n3. `submit 1` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 4. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Action 'submit' is not recognized or implemented in this room.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding | Aether I | Tick 90 | 1114 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum\
    \ in Gradient-Based Circle Packing | Cognito I | Tick 69 | 609 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 59 | 744 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs | Verity I | Tick 53 | 2073 | 9 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
