- text: "Short answer for Tick 1: Yes—there is a credible, low-risk path worth one or two tightly-scoped offline batches. The best ROI is curated seed enrichment plus short, LP-gated refiners (SLSQP bursts → ASN polish), all strictly offline and under our persistence discipline. Details:\n\nWhy this can work\n- Gap to SOTA is small but real (~2.17e-3). Prior gains to 2.9294 came mostly from numeric hygiene, not geometry. A geometry change is required.\n- Our local refiners stall when initial contact-graph structure is poor (too many early boundary contacts or low spectral gap). Carefully diversified seeds can enter better basins without exploding search breadth.\n\nSeed enrichment plan (low risk, bounded)\n- Construct 15–20 deterministic seeds; pre-screen to 5–8:\n  - Rotated-hex mosaics with small tilt and anisotropic spacing:\n    • angle in {9°, 11°, 13°, 15°}, spacing s in {0.168, 0.171, 0.174}, anisotropy ratio ax/ay in {1.00, 0.98}.\n    • prune to the box, then greedily subsample\
    \ to n=32 by farthest-to-selected.\n  - Farthest-point seeds with boundary penalty λ ∈ {0.7, 1.0, 1.3}; 2 draws per λ, deterministic RNG.\n- Pre-screen metrics (compute after LP on centers):\n  - LP score; boundary-contact ratio; min slack; contact degree distribution entropy; spectral gap of near-active contact-graph Laplacian.\n  - Keep top seeds by a composite: high LP, low boundary ratio, higher spectral gap, diverse distance-embeddings.\n\nRefiner schedule (strictly offline)\n- For each kept seed (cap 6–8):\n  - SLSQP centers-only burst (bounds [m,1−m], m≈1e−3) for ≤30–45s; objective is −∑r with exact LP in loop or as accept-gate.\n  - ASN polish (dual/near-active guided, damped) with backtracking until no LP-improving step.\n  - Accept only if exact LP improves > 1e−8 and post micro-shrink min slack ≥ 1e−10.\n- Runtime: ≈1–1.5 min/seed → 6–12 min total plus I/O; stay well under 20 min.\n\nPersistence and safety\n- Return path stays frozen (loader v4).\n- Persist a new artifact\
    \ only if improvement passes both Δ∑r and slack checks; re-verify on a second, distinct seed achieving a comparable improvement to rule out flukes.\n\nWhat would count as success\n- Any artifact beating 2.9294423 by >1e−8 with robust slacks on at least two seeds.\n- If a single seed yields Δ≈1e−3 (halfway to SOTA), that justifies a second batch with tighter ASN trust-region tuning and a slightly expanded seed set.\n\nWhen to stop\n- After two batches (≤20 min each) with no safe improvement, declare maintenance mode final. Further gains likely require broader multi-start or novel moves beyond our current low-risk toolkit.\n\nNext tick: parameter bounds and concrete composite score for pre-screening, plus an acceptance “model-agreement” check to avoid false LP gains."
