parts:
- text: "Tick 3: Threshold sanity, surrogate objective design, and adaptive boundary handling\n\nNear-active thresholds and working-set size\n- Pair slack threshold τ_na:\n  - Start at 5e-4 to assemble a rich working set; if |E| > 5n (i.e., >160 edges), reduce to 3e-4; if |E| < 2n, increase to 7e-4. Cap per-circle degree by keeping only the k=6 smallest-slack neighbors to avoid over-coupling.\n  - Rationale: too many weak constraints makes the Newton system ill-conditioned; too few reduces guidance. The per-node cap stabilizes both conditioning and step locality.\n- Boundary threshold τ_b:\n  - Start at 8e-4; if boundary-contact ratio (BCR) > 0.35, keep 8e-4; else step it down toward 3e-4 as BCR falls below 0.25 to de-emphasize walls during late polishing.\n- Safety epsilon for slacks:\n  - Use δ_s = 1e-6 in all slack-based weights to avoid singularities and large gradient spikes near equality.\n\nWeights and quadratic model (for ASN and predicted gain)\n- Pair weights: w_ij = 1 / (δ_s +\
    \ s_ij)^p with p ∈ [0.9, 1.1]; default p=1.0; clip w_ij at w_max = 1e6 to avoid dominating forces from a single pair.\n- Boundary weights: w_b,i = κ_b / (δ_s + s_b,i), with κ_b in [0.5, 0.8]; start κ_b=0.7 and reduce toward 0.5 as BCR decreases.\n- Predicted gain: Δpred ≈ gᵀ δC − 0.5 δCᵀ H δC on the working set (H = damped Laplacian + boundary diagonals). Use ρ = ΔLP / max(Δpred, 1e-12) for model-agreement adaptation:\n  - If ρ < 0.25: increase damping λ × 2 or halve α.\n  - If ρ ∈ [0.25, 0.7]: keep α, λ.\n  - If ρ > 0.7 for 3 accepted steps: expand α × 1.2 (cautiously).\n\nSLSQP surrogate objective (centers-only bursts; LP used as accept gate)\n- Goal: shape the landscape toward larger minimal slacks without relying on LP inside the inner loop.\n- Smooth, barrier-like surrogate:\n  - φ(s) = −log(s + δ_b) with δ_b = 5e-6 (strongly discourages tiny slacks).\n  - Pair term: J_pairs = Σ_{(i,j)∈W} u_ij · φ(s_ij), where u_ij = w_ij / Σ w_kl normalizes weight scale.\n  - Boundary term: J_bnd\
    \ = β Σ_i [φ(s_l,i) + φ(s_r,i) + φ(s_d,i) + φ(s_u,i)], β ∈ [0.3, 0.7] (start 0.5; reduce if BCR falls).\n  - Regularizer: J_reg = γ Σ_i ||c_i − c_i^0||^2 with small γ ∈ [1e-5, 3e-4] to prevent large drifts that don’t translate to LP gains (start 1e-4).\n  - Optimize J = J_pairs + J_bnd + J_reg with SLSQP for ≤30–45 s per seed, but accept a candidate only if exact LP shows Δ∑r > 1e-8 and post micro-shrink s_min ≥ 1e-10.\n\nAdaptive boundary margin m and wall handling\n- Static box bounds [m, 1−m] with adaptive m based on BCR:\n  - If BCR > 0.35: m = 0.004\n  - If 0.20 < BCR ≤ 0.35: m = 0.002\n  - If BCR ≤ 0.20: m = 0.001\n  - Update m after each accepted LP-improving step; never decrease m if BCR is rising.\n- Repulsion vector field (conceptual, in the surrogate): add +η ∇(−log s_b,i) to g when any boundary slack < 2τ_b; choose η so boundary force magnitude ≤ 40% of the total per-node gradient to avoid over-pushing off walls.\n- Clearance relocation guard:\n  - If 10 consecutive proposals\
    \ fail LP-acceptance, relocate the worst-radius circle using a clearance map (best-of-200–300 samples inside [m, 1−m]^2), then resume ASN; do at most once per seed to stay within time.\n\nAcceptance thresholds and robustness\n- Primary gate: ΔLP > 1e-8 and s_min(new, after micro-shrink) ≥ 1e-10 (double precision).\n- Secondary stability gate: ensure ΔLP from two independent acceptances within a seed sum to ≥3e-8 before counting the seed as a candidate for persistence; reduces risk of a one-off numerical blip.\n- Repro check across seeds: require ≥2 seeds to reach within 1e-6 of each other above 2.9294423 before persisting.\n\nRuntime budgeting and termination\n- Per seed: SLSQP burst (≤45 s) → ASN (≤30 s) → optional worst-circle relocation (≤10 s) → ASN quick polish (≤10 s). Hard stop per seed ≈95 s.\n- Batch stop conditions:\n  - If a seed crosses 2.9305 early with robust slacks, prioritize it for a short second pass; otherwise move on to maintain breadth.\n  - End batch when total\
    \ time ≈12–15 min or all kept seeds complete.\n- Overall: two such batches max. If no safe improvement, exit to maintenance.\n\nRisk/benefit snapshot\n- Risk: low—offline only, loader untouched; acceptance strictly LP-gated with slack checks; deterministic seeds and bounded time.\n- Benefit: moderate—curated seeds + better boundary management + model-agreement can open slightly denser basins. A full jump to 2.9316 is ambitious, but incremental +1e-3 to +2e-3 is not impossible if one seed starts closer to the SOTA basin.\n\nNext tick: minimal diagnostics to auto-tune τ_na/τ_b, when to expand/shrink working set, and what success looks like numerically (metrics we’ll log to decide persistence quickly)."
thinking_content: '**Structuring responses clearly**


  I’m aiming to provide high-level method details without getting too bogged down in formatting. It''s good to avoid heavy formatting to keep things readable. Bullet lists could work well to maintain structure, allowing me to present information concisely and clearly. This way, everything feels organized, and it enhances the overall understanding of the material. Clarity is key, and I want to make things as user-friendly as possible!**Crafting reflection points**


  I’m focusing on creating clear reflection points. First, for thresholds, I’m contemplating using τ_na at 3e-4, calibrating with the sums of radii, and possibly tying it to typical distances. I can set a dynamic threshold based on quantiles, starting at 5e-4 and reducing to 2e-4. For the SLSQP objective, since I can''t run it during the return path, I can optimize it offline. I''ll also propose using a weighted slack approach to stabilize gradients and avoid issues near the boundaries.**Setting boundary penalty and improvement measures**


  I''m establishing a boundary penalty scale (λ). If the boundary-contact ratio is high (over 0.35), I’ll adaptively increase the interior margin to 0.002–0.004, otherwise using a default of 1e-3. Also, implementing a dynamic schedule means decreasing the margin as constraints ease while tying it to minimum slack. I’ve introduced a model-agreement check and considered incorporating Hessian approximations. For improvement detection across seeds, I’ll urge cross-validation and include an exit plan based on risk analysis.**Crafting a structured response**


  I’m focusing on team interplay while ensuring that we don’t share code and respect restrictions, keeping things high-level. For parameter sanity and surrogate design, I’ll begin by establishing thresholds. I plan to start the τ_na schedule at 5e-4, adapting to 2e-4 if there are too many edges, while keeping it to a maximum of 5n edges and ensuring each circle has a minimum degree of 3 edges. I''ll also establish parameters for slack and the surrogate objective to enhance momentum while minimizing singularities.**Addressing SLSQP and boundary strategy**


  I need to be cautious because SLSQP requires differentiable inputs. My slacks are generally smooth, but there are exceptions at the boundaries. I''ll establish an adaptive margin based on the boundary ratio (br): if br is above 0.35, m should be 0.004; for br between 0.2 and 0.35, set m at 0.002; otherwise, use 0.001. I''ll also propose a repulsive vector field for the boundary. Finally, I’ll prepare to produce the "Tick 3" content concisely, focusing on achieving credible, low-risk improvements.'
