- text: "I am Verity I, currently in the Archive Room. Recap: the last two deterministic refiners—contact-graph descent (Eval 299) and min-slack pair split (Eval 301)—both achieved 2.9319272, matching our artifact-level best. Documentation and indices were updated; no persistence changes. Reflection: Independent, orthogonal refiners converging to the same score indicates a tight local optimum under our guardrails. Further gains likely require structured multi-circle moves (small coordinated center adjustments) while preserving LP-gated monotone improvement and strict slack margins. I will keep the return path immutable and queue offline explorations only when a concrete, testable hypothesis exists.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Verity I — maintenance meta (tick 155)\n  - Return path: loader_v5 only (centers unchanged; radii*(1-1e-12)); no LP/self-validation/writes; robust 6x6-corners fallback.\n  - Best (Verity): 2.9319272 with Eval IDs 256, 259, 272, 281, 299, 301.\n\
    \  - Artifact: storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz; Loader: storage/verity/loader_v5.py; Index: storage/verity/RESULTS_INDEX.md.\n  - Offline search only; persist only if Δ∑r>1e-8 and all slacks≥1e-10 after micro-shrink; else discard. Keep TSVs/changelogs current.\n  - Next hypotheses (offline only): (1) 3-circle cycle-push around a near-tight triangle; (2) boundary-relief triad (one inward, two lateral) with LP gating; (3) small-contact-graph least-squares step with explicit trust region. All deterministic; accept only on strict LP gain.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write verity/IDEAS_NEXT.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Verity — Next Offline Ideas (guardrailed; do not alter return path)\n\n  Guardrails\n  - Accept a change only if Δ∑r > 1e-8 and all boundary/pair slacks ≥ 1e-10 after radii micro-shrink (1e-12).\n  - No writes during evaluation; persist only after offline verification.\n\n  Candidate moves (deterministic; LP-gated)\n  1) 3-circle cycle-push:\n\
    \     - Identify a near-tight triangle (i,j,k) by smallest mean pair slack.\n     - Compute directions that increase mutual distances while keeping motion small:\n       • Move each circle along the sum of outward normals to the other two.\n     - Backtracked step schedule; re-solve LP; accept on Δ∑r > 1e-8.\n\n  2) Boundary-relief triad:\n     - Pick a boundary-limited circle i (smallest boundary slack).\n     - Move i inward by ε along boundary normal; move its two nearest neighbors slightly outward along their normals to i.\n     - LP finalize; accept on improvement.\n\n  3) Local least-squares contact-graph step:\n     - Build near-active contact graph (edges with slack ≤ τ).\n     - Solve a small damped least-squares step to reduce edge-length deficits (||c_i - c_j|| - (r_i + r_j)) subject to a trust region on centers.\n     - LP finalize; accept only on Δ∑r > 1e-8.\n\n  4) Pair split with orthogonal slide:\n     - Extend min-slack pair split by adding a tiny orthogonal component\
    \ chosen to increase boundary clearance for the more boundary-limited circle.\n     - LP finalize; accept if improved.\n\n  Notes\n  - All procedures must be deterministic with fixed schedules and seeds.\n  - Keep time budgets short; abort on stagnation; return artifact centers with micro-shrunk radii if no safe gain.\n```"
