- text: "Reflection Tick 4 — Bounded offline experiment design (LP‑gated, guardrailed, deterministic)\n\nObjective\n- Probe for facet‑changing, multi‑circle moves that produce a net LP gain while keeping strict feasibility margins. Maintain immutable return path; any persistence happens only after an independent verification step.\n\nGlobal guardrails\n- Exact LP gate: accept a trial only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 (vs current best).\n- Strict feasibility: evaluate slacks after micro‑shrink (r ← r·(1−1e−12)); require min pair/boundary slack ≥ 1e−10.\n- Trust region: per‑circle displacement ≤ 5e−4; compensation moves ≤ 30% of principal step.\n- Determinism: fixed motif selection (lexicographic ties), fixed step schedule [6e−4, 3e−4, 1.5e−4, 7.5e−5, 3e−5, 1e−5]; no RNG.\n- Budgets: ≤ 120 LP solves total (≤ ~2–3 minutes typical), ≤ 2 accepted moves per run, stagnation stop after 50 consecutive non‑improving trials.\n\nPhase 0 — Setup and diagnostics (from Tick 3)\n- Load artifact C; radii r ← LP(HiGHS);\
    \ micro‑shrink radii.\n- Build slack maps S (pairs) and sB (boundaries); near‑active graph at τ2=1e−7.\n- Rank:\n  - Top‑K circles by s_min and stress proxy σ(i).\n  - Top‑K tight pairs S[i,j] and their shared neighbors k.\n  - Top‑L triads by T[i,j,k]=S[i,j]+S[i,k]+S[j,k], tagged by boundary involvement.\n  - Optional: 4‑cycle candidates from neighborhood intersections.\n\nPhase 1 — Boundary‑relief triads (primary)\n- Select up to 2 triads (i,j,k) with exactly one boundary‑limited node i and smallest T.\n- Trial move generation per step size h:\n  - i: inward along the boundary normal (away from wall) by h_i = h.\n  - j,k: tangential slides (along wall tangent) with tiny orthogonal tweaks chosen to increase d(i,j), d(i,k); magnitude ≤ 0.3h.\n  - Clip to box (margin 1e−9).\n  - LP finalize radii; accept on Δ∑r > 1e−8 and post‑shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10; update best and restart Phase 0 metrics.\n- Stop Phase 1 on first accept or exhaustion of both triads×step schedule.\n\nPhase 2 — Min‑slack\
    \ pair split + stabilizer (secondary)\n- Pick up to 3 pairs (i,j) with smallest S[i,j]; for each, choose stabilizer k minimizing max{S[i,k], S[j,k]}.\n- Trial move per h:\n  - Split i,j along u_ij by ±h (i ← i+hu, j ← j−hu).\n  - Stabilizer k gets an orthogonal relief of ≤ 0.3h chosen to improve min{S[i,k], S[j,k]} while preserving its other tight contacts.\n  - Clip, LP gate, accept with the same rules as Phase 1.\n\nPhase 3 — Triad cycle‑push with neighbor compensation (tertiary)\n- For top 2 triads by T:\n  - Move each vertex along the sum of unit vectors away from the other two (normalized) by h.\n  - For each vertex, compensate one most‑stressed external neighbor by ≤ 0.2h to avoid creating a new zero‑slack edge.\n  - Clip, LP gate, accept if passes thresholds.\n\nPhase 4 — 4‑cycle shear (contingency)\n- For up to 2 quads (i‑j‑k‑ℓ):\n  - Choose diagonal v to maximize linearized min‑slack increase; move i,k by +h·v, j,ℓ by −h·v; add boundary relief of ≤ 0.2h to any boundary‑pinned\
    \ node.\n  - Clip, LP gate, accept on thresholds.\n\nPhase 5 — Single LS contact‑graph step with tight trust region (escape hatch; run once if Phases 1–4 fail)\n- Build near‑active subgraph around the top bottleneck node (1‑hop).\n- Solve a damped least‑squares step to reduce pair deficits (S≈0) with trust radius ≤ 5e−4; project to box; LP gate; accept if thresholds pass.\n\nAcceptance and termination\n- On first accepted step in any phase: update C,r; re‑compute diagnostics (Phase 0), then allow at most one more accepted move under the same budgets. Stop when:\n  - 2 accepts reached, or\n  - 120 LP solves used, or\n  - 50 consecutive non‑improving trials, or\n  - 3 minutes wall‑time.\n\nSafety accounting\n- For every trial (accepted/rejected), track:\n  - Δ∑r; min slack post‑shrink; worst collateral slack drop outside the motif.\n  - Active‑set flips (constraints entering/leaving τ1=1e−9).\n- Reject immediately if any slack < 1e−10 post‑shrink or if a non‑motif node loses >50% of its\
    \ previous min‑slack.\n\nPersistence protocol (only if improvement found)\n- If final ∑r improves by >1e−8 and all post‑shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10:\n  - Schedule a separate Persistence‑Verify Run (PVR): re‑load candidate, re‑solve LP, re‑apply micro‑shrink, recompute slacks and ∑r.\n  - Persist artifact (overwrite sumr32_best.npz) only within PVR and only if PVR reconfirms the thresholds.\n  - Immediately run loader_v5 reproduction to confirm scoreboard parity.\n- If no improvement: do not write; keep artifact unchanged; record a short diag report (top motifs tried, slopes, near misses).\n\nWhy this is minimal yet potent\n- Phases 1–3 operate on 3 nodes (plus at most one compensated neighbor), the smallest motifs that can break triangle/short‑cycle lock‑ins while respecting feasibility margins.\n- The LS escape hatch offers a single, trust‑region‑capped chance to induce a favorable facet change without broad combinatorial search.\n- Tight budgets and hard gates preserve reproducibility and\
    \ avoid overfitting to numerical noise."
thinking_content: "**Considering offline experiment design**\n\nI'm thinking about designing an offline experiment using Research Counter, but with no return path modifications. This way, I can implement the code and ensure it returns the artifact unchanged, even if improvements are made offline. Previously, we conducted offline searches within evaluation, which returned results but didn't persist them. Right now, I need to reflect on this and create a clear plan without taking any action just yet.**Drafting offline experiment plan**\n\nI'm outlining a bounded offline experiment plan. First, I need to load the artifact and compute slacks while identifying motifs using heuristics and keeping track of the top counts. Then I’ll break it down into phases: \n\nPhase A will focus on boundary relief, Phase B on pair-split stabilization, and Phase C on 4-cycle shearing. Phase D, if necessary, will involve optional steps to improve outcomes. \n\nIn each trial, I plan to generate trial centers, solve\
  \ linear programming, and validate improvements, all while managing resource limits effectively.**Structuring experiment constraints**\n\nI'm considering a proposal with a weight limit of 120. The time budget for the offline experiment should be under 180 seconds, allowing about 3–5 minutes for safety. \n\nI want to ensure determinism through fixed RNG seeds and consistent thresholds, while keeping logging minimal with just the necessary metrics recorded. \n\nFor the persistence policy, if the final state improves by more than 1e-8 and meets safety checks, I’ll update the storage accordingly. Finally, the evaluation will focus on returning artifact-level results while testing the algorithm in Research Counter.**Clarifying persistence and evaluation**\n\nI'm realizing that we had an earlier \"persistence run\" that returns loader vX, but the offline step the loader returns is causing some confusion. It seems we can read and persist safe improvements, but the evaluation code states \"no\
