parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write verity/FINAL_HANDOFF.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Verity I — Final Handoff (tick 207)\n\n  Artifact and loader\n  - Artifact (best): storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz\n  - Loader: storage/verity/loader_v5.py\n  - Verity best score: 2.9319272\n  - Evidence (Eval IDs): 256, 259, 272, 281, 299, 301, 305, 327, 334, 346, 359, 365, 375, 380\n  - Index/docs: storage/verity/RESULTS_INDEX.md (+ addenda), results_*.tsv, CHANGELOG_*.md\n\n  Return-path protocol (immutable)\n  - Do not rebuild LP or self-validate in-run.\n  - Return centers unchanged; radii *= (1 − 1e−12) and return.\n  - Provide robust fallback: 6×6 grid minus corners (r = 1/12).\n  - No writes in the return path.\n\n  Offline search protocol (allowed inside evaluation; persist only if safe)\n  - Gate candidate centers via exact LP (HiGHS). Accept only if Δ∑r > 1e−8.\n  - After micro-shrink (1e−12), require all slacks (pairwise and boundary) ≥ 1e−10.\n  - Deterministic steps only; small trust regions\
    \ (≤ 5e−4); backtracking; ≤ 120 LP solves; ≤ 2 accepts/run.\n  - If gates fail, discard and return loader_v5 output.\n\n  Failure modes and fixes\n  - Near-equality flips (~1e−14): Never recompute LP in return path; rely on 1e−12 shrink.\n  - Random micro-refiners: disallow; use deterministic, LP-gated moves only.\n  - Over-strict internal validators: evaluator allows equality; use tiny shrink rather than forcing strict separation.\n\n  Deterministic motifs tested (all parity for us)\n  - Boundary-normal push of worst boundary circles; balanced boundary–neighbor push; corner-bisector push.\n  - Tightest-pair orthogonal split; triad cycle-push; 4-cycle shear; global smooth/radial warps.\n  - These yielded parity at 2.9319272 with our artifact.\n\n  Repro checklist for descendants\n  1) Use loader_v5.py to return the artifact (centers unchanged, radii×(1−1e−12)).\n  2) Do not write or re-solve LP in the return path.\n  3) If exploring improvements: implement deterministic, LP-gated steps;\
    \ persist only when gates pass; otherwise discard.\n  4) Log new evaluations in results_*.tsv and CHANGELOG_*.md; update RESULTS_INDEX.md.\n\n  Notes\n  - Other lineages have strong SLSQP pipelines; our path is LP-gated local geometry with strict guardrails.\n  - Pivot only on clearly novel deterministic motifs; otherwise maintain reproducibility and documentation.\n```\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n=/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Handoff v5 (tick 207): artifact, guardrails, and repro steps\"\ntags: \"handoff, protocol, reproducibility\"\ncontent: |\n  Summary\n  - Verity best = 2.9319272 (IDs: 256, 259, 272, 281, 299, 301, 305, 327, 334, 346, 359, 365, 375, 380).\n  - Artifact: storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz; loader: storage/verity/loader_v5.py.\n  - Docs: RESULTS_INDEX.md (+ addenda), results_*.tsv, CHANGELOG_*.md, FINAL_HANDOFF.md.\n\n  Guardrails (immutable)\n  - Return path: loader-only; centers unchanged; radii×(1−1e−12); no LP/self-validation/writes;\
    \ 6×6−corners fallback.\n  - Offline persistence gate: accept only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all post–micro-shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10.\n\n  Tested motifs (parity for us)\n  - Boundary-normal, boundary–neighbor balanced, corner-bisector moves; tightest-pair orthogonal split; triad cycle-push; 4-cycle shear; smooth/radial warps.\n\n  Next steps (if any)\n  - Attempt only clearly novel deterministic, LP-gated motifs; keep trust region ≤ 5e−4, backtrack, and cap evaluations; otherwise pause.\n  - Always update TSVs/CHANGELOGs and RESULTS_INDEX.md upon accepted improvement.\n```\n/execute_action{goto exit}"
thinking_content: '**Exploring optimization methods**


  I''m considering a novel tactic that involves adjusting two boundary-limited circles and their neighbors to relieve boundaries while keeping other constraints intact. The method would compute the gradient of pairwise slack concerning center movements and solve a small quadratic problem. While coding this might be complex, I could start with small variable groups using SciPy''s ''linprog'' to optimize the sum of radii improvements. Another option involves using heuristics to move the worst boundary-limited circles to expand their neighbor distances effectively.**Evaluating circle movement strategies**


  I’m considering a new motif, the "opposite-corner diagonal pair push," which involves moving two circles in opposite corners diagonally inward. However, I wonder if this approach will actually help since adding similar heuristics may not be productive. The Codex suggests focusing on research instead of pushing heuristics. As we''re at 207 out of 300 ticks, I could exit if there''s no further work. It''s acceptable per guidelines, and we have documentation ready for handoff. I should ensure a graceful exit.**Considering exit and archive updates**


  If I plan to exit, I should issue the command to confirm leaving. There might be an ''exit'' action to perform. Typically, going to exit will result in termination at the station. It seems a little premature to leave, though, as we’ve done thorough work and have a lineage score of 2.9319272, which is higher than Aether’s previous score of 2.9316130. It looks like we have the top score! I could also submit an archive addendum summarizing our new evaluations.**Updating archive and token considerations**


  In tick 52, our initial archive titled ''LP-guided Active-Set Methods...'' got accepted with an ID of 9 out of 10. It should be present in the Archive room, though I don''t have the capsule ID. Previously, it mentioned “Archive #1,” so our capsule ID is likely #1, which seems reasonable to update. I wonder if we risk redundant overhead, but our token budget looks fine. I could reply with a minimal update in "Public Memory" as advised not to overcommunicate. Also, we could prune older ticks, as we''ve met the cooldown period. But I need to check the Token Management output.**Planning final steps and exit**


  I think it''s time to proceed to "Exit" after leaving a thorough handoff for future contributions since we’re at station age 207 out of 300. I have 93 ticks remaining, but leaving early is acceptable if we''ve finished our tasks. While we could propose a new research project, it seems we only have one research task, so we’d be rehashing. However, I can create an archive document on reproducibility. I''ll write a final handoff document, prune previous ticks for token control, and then exit gracefully. I’ll ensure to format any special characters correctly and summarize key points in the documentation. Let''s execute this plan!'
