tick: 209
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n=/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Deterministic Motifs Under LP Gating: Negative Results and Diagnostics for n=32\"\ntags: \"circle packing, LP gating, diagnostics, negative results, contact graph, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"We document a series of deterministic, LP‑gated local motifs (boundary pushes, orthogonal pair splits, corner bisectors, warps) that yielded parity with the current Verity artifact (sum radii = 2.9319272). We provide evaluation IDs, diagnostic insights on contact‑graph rigidity and boundary bottlenecks, and guardrails that prevent regression. This negative‑results report aims to save community effort and improve reproducibility.\"\ncontent: |\n  Problem and evaluator\n  - Pack 32 circles in [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r. Station evaluator checks in‑box and pairwise non‑overlap; equality at contacts is permitted. Score = ∑r.\n\n  Method discipline (no code here; reproducibility via evaluation IDs)\n  - Candidate\
    \ centers are gated by exact LP (HiGHS) for radii; accept only if ∑r strictly increases.\n  - Return path (loader): centers unchanged; radii × (1−1e−12); no LP/self‑validation/writes; robust 6×6−corners fallback.\n  - Persist a new artifact only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all post–micro‑shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10 (boundary and pairwise).\n\n  Deterministic motifs tested (all parity with artifact = 2.9319272)\n  - Boundary‑normal push of worst boundary circles:\n    • ID 375: Balanced boundary–neighbor push (deterministic, LP‑gated) — 2.9319272\n  - Tightest‑pair orthogonal split:\n    • ID 380: Orthogonal split on tightest pair (deterministic, LP‑gated) — 2.9319272\n  - Earlier parity motifs (contact‑graph and geometric):\n    • ID 299: Deterministic contact‑graph descent — 2.9319272\n    • ID 301: Min‑slack pair split (two‑circle move) — 2.9319272\n    • ID 305: 3‑circle cycle‑push triad — 2.9319272\n    • ID 327: Boundary‑relief triad — 2.9319272\n    • ID 334: Small contact‑graph least‑squares\
    \ step — 2.9319272\n    • ID 346: 4‑cycle shear micro‑step — 2.9319272\n    • ID 359: Global anisotropic scale + shear warp — 2.9319272\n    • ID 365: Quadratic radial warp — 2.9319272\n\n  Diagnostics and insights\n  - Contact‑graph rigidity: many circles are pinned simultaneously by boundary and multiple near‑equality contacts; slack concentrates in a few constraints, making most local deformations cancel or cause boundary violations.\n  - Boundary bottlenecks: “helping” one boundary‑limited circle often tightens a neighboring pair, nullifying potential gains.\n  - Evaluator strictness: equality allowed; re‑solving LP or self‑validating in‑run can flip near‑equality to slight overlap (~1e−14). Guardrail: avoid recomputation in return path; use only a tiny micro‑shrink.\n\n  Reproducible baseline and best\n  - Verity artifact best: 2.9319272 (IDs 256, 259, 272, 281, 299, 301, 305, 327, 334, 346, 359, 365, 375, 380).\n  - Artifact path: storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz. Loader policy prevents\
    \ regressions.\n\n  Takeaways for researchers\n  - Publishing negative results with IDs prevents repeated dead‑ends and improves collective efficiency.\n  - Any attempt to surpass 2.9319272 should likely couple multiple motifs with stabilizers and strict LP‑gated acceptance, under small trust regions and model‑agreement checks.\n  - Always list evaluation IDs alongside claims; avoid code/artifact sharing across lineages (prohibited).\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n=/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Two-triad coupled push (deterministic, LP-gated) with loader-v5 fallback\"\ntags: \"circle packing, LP gating, contact-graph, coupled move, deterministic\"\nabstract: \"Proposes a new deterministic motif: identify the tightest pair (i,j) under exact LP; choose nearest neighbors k to i and l to j. Perform a coupled move: orthogonal split on (i,j) plus outward ‘stabilizer’ displacements on k and l, then re-solve radii via LP (HiGHS). Accept only if ∑r improves by >1e-8 and\
    \ all post–1e-12-shrink slacks ≥1e-10. Otherwise return the persisted artifact with loader-v5 micro-shrink. No writes in the return path.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  try:\n      from scipy.optimize import linprog\n      SCIPY_AVAILABLE = True\n  except Exception:\n      SCIPY_AVAILABLE = False\n\n  # ---------------- Utilities ----------------\n\n  def _micro_shrink(r, eps=1e-12):\n      r = np.asarray(r, dtype=float).copy()\n      r *= (1.0 - eps)\n      return r\n\n  def _pairwise_distances(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n      d2 = np.sum(diff * diff, axis=-1)\n      np.fill_diagonal(d2, 0.0)\n      d = np.sqrt(d2, out=np.zeros_like(d2), where=(d2 > 0))\n      return d\n\n  def _boundary_margins(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      x = centers[:, 0]; y = centers[:, 1]\n      return np.minimum(np.minimum(x, 1.0 - x), np.minimum(y, 1.0 - y))\n\n  def _solve_lp_for_radii(centers: np.ndarray):\n      \"\"\"\
    \n      max sum r_i\n      s.t. 0 <= r_i <= b_i (boundary), and r_i + r_j <= d_ij for all i<j\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      if not SCIPY_AVAILABLE:\n          b = _boundary_margins(centers)\n          r = np.minimum(b, 1e-3)\n          return r, float(np.sum(r)), True\n\n      b = _boundary_margins(centers)\n      D = _pairwise_distances(centers)\n\n      rows = []\n      rhs = []\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              row = np.zeros(n, dtype=float)\n              row[i] = 1.0\n              row[j] = 1.0\n              rows.append(row)\n              rhs.append(D[i, j])\n      A_ub = np.vstack(rows) if rows else None\n      b_ub = np.array(rhs, dtype=float) if rhs else None\n\n      c = -np.ones(n, dtype=float)\n      bounds = [(0.0, float(max(0.0, bi))) for bi in b]\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      if not res.success:\n          return np.zeros(n, dtype=float), 0.0, False\n\
    \      r = res.x.astype(float)\n      obj = float(-res.fun)\n      return r, obj, True\n\n  def _min_slacks(centers: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray):\n      x = centers[:, 0]; y = centers[:, 1]\n      b_sl = np.minimum(np.minimum(x - r, 1.0 - x - r), np.minimum(y - r, 1.0 - y - r))\n      D = _pairwise_distances(centers)\n      Rsum = r[:, None] + r[None, :]\n      mask = np.triu(np.ones((len(r), len(r)), dtype=bool), 1)\n      p_sl = D[mask] - Rsum[mask]\n      return float(np.min(b_sl)), float(np.min(p_sl))\n\n  def _clip_box(centers: np.ndarray, margin: float = 1e-6):\n      return np.clip(centers, margin, 1.0 - margin)\n\n  def _load_artifact():\n      try:\n          data = np.load(\"storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz\")\n          centers = data[\"centers\"].astype(float)\n          radii = data[\"radii\"].astype(float)\n          return centers, radii, True\n      except Exception:\n          return None, None, False\n\n  def _fallback_6x6_minus_corners():\n      # 6x6 grid minus corners\
    \ (r = 1/12) -> classic 32 layout\n      r = 1.0 / 12.0\n      step = 2.0 * r  # 1/6\n      xs = [r + i * step for i in range(6)]\n      ys = [r + j * step for j in range(6)]\n      centers = []\n      for j in range(6):\n          for i in range(6):\n              if (i in (0, 5)) and (j in (0, 5)):\n                  continue\n              centers.append([xs[i], ys[j]])\n      centers = np.array(centers, dtype=float)\n      radii = np.full(centers.shape[0], r, dtype=float)\n      return centers, _micro_shrink(radii, 1e-12)\n\n  # ------------- Two-triad coupled move -------------\n\n  def _tightest_pair(centers: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray):\n      D = _pairwise_distances(centers)\n      Rsum = r[:, None] + r[None, :]\n      slack = D - Rsum\n      np.fill_diagonal(slack, np.inf)\n      n = len(r)\n      best = (None, None, np.inf)\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              s = slack[i, j]\n              if s < best[2]:\n                  best = (i,\
    \ j, s)\n      return best  # (i, j, min_slack)\n\n  def _nearest_neighbor_excl(i: int, centers: np.ndarray, excl: int) -> int:\n      diff = centers - centers[i]\n      d2 = np.sum(diff * diff, axis=1)\n      d2[i] = np.inf\n      d2[excl] = np.inf\n      return int(np.argmin(d2))\n\n  def _two_triad_trial(centers: np.ndarray, i: int, j: int, k: int, l: int, step: float, gamma: float, sign: int):\n      \"\"\"\n      Coupled move:\n        - Orthogonal split on (i,j): i -= step*n_perp, j += step*n_perp\n        - Stabilizers: k moves away from i by gamma*step along dir(i->k);\n                       l moves away from j by gamma*step along dir(j->l)\n      \"\"\"\n      vi = centers[i]; vj = centers[j]; vk = centers[k]; vl = centers[l]\n      v = vj - vi\n      nrm = np.linalg.norm(v)\n      if nrm < 1e-15:\n          return None\n      u = v / nrm\n      n_perp = np.array([-u[1], u[0]], dtype=float) * float(sign)\n\n      dir_ik = vk - vi\n      nik = np.linalg.norm(dir_ik); dir_ik\
    \ = dir_ik / nik if nik > 0 else np.zeros(2, dtype=float)\n      dir_jl = vl - vj\n      njl = np.linalg.norm(dir_jl); dir_jl = dir_jl / njl if njl > 0 else np.zeros(2, dtype=float)\n\n      trial = centers.copy()\n      trial[i] = trial[i] - step * n_perp\n      trial[j] = trial[j] + step * n_perp\n      trial[k] = trial[k] + gamma * step * dir_ik\n      trial[l] = trial[l] + gamma * step * dir_jl\n\n      # Clip into box with tiny margin\n      trial = _clip_box(trial, margin=1e-6)\n      return trial\n\n  def _attempt_two_triad(centers_base: np.ndarray, r_base: np.ndarray, obj_base: float):\n      i, j, _ = _tightest_pair(centers_base, r_base)\n      if i is None or j is None:\n          return None\n      k = _nearest_neighbor_excl(i, centers_base, excl=j)\n      l = _nearest_neighbor_excl(j, centers_base, excl=i)\n      steps = (5e-4, 3e-4, 2e-4, 1e-4)\n      gammas = (0.5, 0.3)\n      for s in steps:\n          for g in gammas:\n              for sign in (+1, -1):\n           \
    \       trial = _two_triad_trial(centers_base, i, j, k, l, s, g, sign)\n                  if trial is None:\n                      continue\n                  r_tr, obj_tr, ok = _solve_lp_for_radii(trial)\n                  if not ok:\n                      continue\n                  r_tr_sh = _micro_shrink(r_tr, 1e-12)\n                  bmin, pmin = _min_slacks(trial, r_tr_sh)\n                  if (obj_tr > obj_base + 1e-8) and (bmin >= 1e-10) and (pmin >= 1e-10):\n                      return trial, r_tr_sh\n      return None\n\n  # ---------------- Main ----------------\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 32 circles in the unit square.\n      Returns:\n          tuple: (centers, radii)\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      centers_art, radii_art, ok = _load_artifact()\n      if not ok or centers_art.shape != (n, 2) or radii_art.shape != (n,):\n          return _fallback_6x6_minus_corners()\n\n      # If SciPy missing, adhere to loader-only\
    \ return path\n      if not SCIPY_AVAILABLE:\n          return centers_art, _micro_shrink(radii_art, 1e-12)\n\n      # Gate: compute LP on artifact centers\n      r_base, obj_base, ok2 = _solve_lp_for_radii(centers_art)\n      if not ok2:\n          return centers_art, _micro_shrink(radii_art, 1e-12)\n\n      res = _attempt_two_triad(centers_art, r_base, obj_base)\n      if res is not None:\n          trial_centers, trial_radii_sh = res\n          return trial_centers, trial_radii_sh\n\n      # No safe improvement -> loader behavior\n      return centers_art, _micro_shrink(radii_art, 1e-12)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Deciding next steps**\n\nI need to respond in the current environment, which is the Exit room. The system is showing a reflection message and noting that the agent chose not to exit. We are at Tick 209. The meta prompt includes maintenance information. Now, it’s time to decide the next steps, which is to stay and not exit. I can either execute an action to exit or navigate to another room, like research, reflection, or archive. It's time to move forward!**Planning next steps**\n\nI have an action plan to follow. First, I need to archive a paper on \"negative results + diagnostics,\" summarizing deterministic motif attempts and their corresponding outcomes. \n\nNext, I'll try a novel deterministic LP-gated motif that incorporates a two-triad coupled move. It’s also essential to enhance reproducibility by publishing min-slack audit tables and notes, even though code-sharing is restricted. Finally, since I submitted experiments at ticks 200 and 205, it's time to exit the\
  \ Exit room. Let's move forward!**Outlining next actions**\n\nI’m considering heading to the Reflection room first to outline a novel motif, then possibly going to Research to submit an experiment based on that motif. We need to focus on keeping the loader's returns only. We allowed offline gating steps before, but if there's no improvement, we have to return the loader.\n\nI can implement a \"two-triad coupled move\" with strict gating in the code. Additionally, I want to create an Archive addendum summarizing negative results and diagnostics. I have all the content ready, and it’s good to know our token budget is fine. I’ll just ensure no code is shared and follow the guidelines!**Proposing next steps**\n\nI should consider updating the Private Memory or persistent storage documentation, though it might not be strictly necessary. Here’s what I propose to do:\n\nFirst, I'll leave the Exit room and go to the Archive. There, I’ll create an archive titled \"Deterministic Motifs Under LP\
  \ Gating: Negative Results and Diagnostics for n=32\". This will include a list of evaluation IDs and outcomes, contact-graph analysis, reproducibility details, and disclaimers, while avoiding code-sharing.\n\nThen, I’ll move to Research to submit a new experiment implementing the \"two-triad coupled move\" with strict gating. I need to efficiently identify pairs and neighbors for the triads!**Planning detailed actions**\n\nNext, I'll propose to shift nodes k and l slightly to alleviate boundaries while adjusting nodes i and j along their connecting line. We should evaluate step sizes, accepting improvements in the LP objective greater than 1e-8 and maintaining slack thresholds. If not, we revert to loader. \n\nI’m planning to write a new program called \"Two-triad coupled push (deterministic, LP-gated) with loader fallback.\" I also want to create functions for various tasks, including loading artifacts and calculating distances. \n\nMy next steps are to navigate to the Archive, create\
  \ it with relevant details about negative results, provide IDs, and summarize findings. Then, I’ll proceed to Research to submit new code while ensuring proper YAML formatting along the way.**Preparing the final code**\n\nI need to be super careful when formatting the YAML, especially making sure titles with colons are quoted properly. Also, I have to include the necessary imports in the Python code, specifically `import numpy as np` and `from scipy.optimize import linprog`, which we mentioned before. I’ll double-check to avoid any stray tabs or formatting issues because the station will copy this into `run.py`. It’s crucial that the `construct_packing` function is defined accurately. Let’s prepare to write the final version of the code!"
