tick: 224
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n=/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Method and Rigorous Evaluation\"\ntags: \"circle packing, LP gating, local move, baseline, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"We define and evaluate a single deterministic method—the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)—under exact LP gating and strict feasibility checks. We report full-precision results with evaluation IDs from two initializations (grid 6×6−corners and a strong artifact) and compare against baselines. OSM does not improve the score under our safety criteria; we discuss implications and provide clear pseudocode to enable reproduction.\"\ncontent: |\n  Problem and evaluator\n  - Pack n=32 circles in [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r. The Station’s evaluator enforces positivity, in‑box, and pairwise non‑overlap and returns the sum of radii. Equality at contacts is permitted.\n\n  Method: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)\n  - Input: centers C ∈ R^{32×2}. Compute\
    \ radii r by exact LP (HiGHS): maximize ∑ r_i s.t. 0 ≤ r_i ≤ boundary(C_i), r_i + r_j ≤ ||C_i−C_j||.\n  - Tightest pair: pick (i*, j*) minimizing slack s_ij = ||C_i−C_j|| − (r_i + r_j) using the LP radii r.\n  - Step schedule S = {5e−4, 3e−4, 2e−4, 1e−4}; orientations T = {+1, −1}.\n  - For s ∈ S, t ∈ T:\n      1) u = (C_{j*}−C_{i*}) / ||C_{j*}−C_{i*}||; n_perp = t · (−u_y, u_x).\n      2) Trial centers C' = C; C'_{i*} ← C'_{i*} − s·n_perp; C'_{j*} ← C'_{j*} + s·n_perp; clip C' to [ε, 1−ε], ε=1e−6.\n      3) Solve LP on C' to get r'; apply micro‑shrink r'' = (1−1e−12)·r'.\n      4) Compute min boundary and pairwise slacks on (C', r''). Accept iff:\n         • ∑r' > ∑r + 1e−8, and\n         • min_slack_boundary ≥ 1e−10 and min_slack_pairwise ≥ 1e−10.\n      5) If accepted, return (C', r''); else continue schedule.\n  - If no step is accepted, return the base (C, micro‑shrunk r).\n  - Determinism: fixed schedule, no randomness.\n\n  Experimental setup\n  - Exact LP: HiGHS via scipy.optimize.linprog.\n\
    \  - Initializations:\n    • Grid: 6×6 grid minus the 4 corners (“grid 6×6−corners”).\n    • Artifact: Verity loader artifact centers (storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz).\n  - Safety gates: as in the method above. All runs deterministic.\n\n  Results (full precision with evaluation IDs)\n  - Baselines:\n    • ID 395 — Grid 6×6−corners + LP + 1e−12 micro‑shrink: 2.666666666664\n    • ID 396 — Artifact centers + deterministic global jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=1) + LP + micro‑shrink: 2.9275900457126927\n    • References (artifact loader): ID 272 = 2.9319272; ID 281 = 2.9319272\n  - OSM (this method):\n    • ID 399 — OSM from Grid 6×6−corners: 2.666666666664 (no accepted step; equals grid baseline)\n    • ID 380 — OSM from Artifact: 2.9319272179903653 (no accepted step; equals artifact loader)\n\n  Interpretation\n  - Under strict LP‑gated acceptance and post‑shrink slack checks, OSM does not improve either from a simple grid or from a strong artifact. This suggests the orthogonal split alone is\
    \ insufficient to unlock slack in the near‑rigid contact structure; stabilizers or multi‑circle couplings may be required.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - Deterministic procedure; exact acceptance rules and thresholds specified; all evidence reported with evaluation IDs and full scores.\n  - No code or artifacts are shared across lineages; results can be verified by re‑running the method as defined.\n\n  Future work\n  - Explore coupled motifs (e.g., pair‑split with stabilizers, triad cycle‑push with compensation) with the same LP‑gated acceptance and safety thresholds.\n  - Model‑agreement checks and adaptive trust regions to avoid futile micro‑moves near rigidity.\n"
thinking_content: "**Preparing archive submission**\n\nI’m getting ready to submit an archive titled \"Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32-circle packing: method and evaluation.\" In the abstract, I’ll describe OSM's implementation with exact LP gating and acceptance criteria, evaluating it from various IDs. I need to ensure we don’t include actual code but can use pseudocode instead. Additionally, I’ll provide a results table in plaintext. While there's some mention of Token Management, I’ll stay focused on crafting the archive content first.**Confirming precision for OSM**\n\nI’m checking the score for ID 380, which is 2.9319272 according to the table, but I wonder if earlier messages might have been truncated. I see that the full precision value for ID 380 is actually \"2.9319272179903653,\" and the same goes for ID 388. So, it looks like I need to use these exact values from evaluations. For IDs 272 and 281, they show \"2.9319272,\" which is truncated to seven decimals, but it’s still\
  \ good to include what I have while aiming for full precision.**Preparing capsule content**\n\nI’m organizing the capsule content, starting with the grid baseline of 2.666666666664, which I’ll present fully, and the jitter baseline of 2.9275900457126927. \n\nUnder the Methodology section, I'll define OSM and explain how to compute the 'tightest pair' by using specific steps and schedules. Then, for the Experimental setup, I’ll describe the two starting states of a 6x6 grid minus corners, using predetermined artifact centers. \n\nFinally, I’ll include a results table with evaluation IDs, starting with ID 395, showing the grid LP micro-shrink value.**Compiling evaluation results**\n\nI’m summarizing the evaluation results for ID 396, where the artifact global jitter baseline is 2.9275900457126927. The OSM values show no improvement over the baseline: ID 399 (2.666666666664) and ID 380 (2.9319272179903653) are equal to their respective artifact loaders. In the discussion, it’s clear the OSM\
  \ approach alone isn’t enough, highlighting the need for micro-shrink and gating for safety. \n\nFor future work, I’d like to couple systems with stabilizers. I should also record the results and prepare to create an archive in the right location. Maybe updating the storage isn’t urgent, but I’ll note the to-do in private memory."
