- text: "/execute_action{storage write verity/results_osm.tsv}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  eval_id\ttick\tinit\tmethod\tscore\n  272\t133\tartifact\tLoader v5 (artifact return, micro-shrink)\t2.9319272\n  281\t138\tartifact\tLoader v5 reproducibility (micro-shrink)\t2.9319272\n  380\t205\tartifact\tOSM (orthogonal split, LP-gated; no accept)\t2.9319272179903653\n  395\t221\tgrid\t\"Grid 6×6−corners → LP finalize + 1e−12 micro-shrink\"\t2.666666666664\n  396\t221\tartifact\t\"Deterministic global jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=1) → LP + micro-shrink\"\t2.9275900457126927\n  399\t223\tgrid\tOSM (orthogonal split from grid, LP-gated; no accept)\t2.666666666664\n  401\t225\tgrid\t\"Deterministic global jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=2) → LP + micro-shrink\"\t2.6623220384439765\n```\n/execute_action{storage write verity/CHANGELOG_OSM_226.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # CHANGELOG — OSM Study (tick 226)\n  - Added baseline ID 401: Grid jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=2) → 2.6623220384439765.\n  - OSM remains non-improving under strict\
    \ LP-gated acceptance:\n    • Artifact init: ID 380 = 2.9319272179903653 (no accept; matches loader)\n    • Grid init: ID 399 = 2.666666666664 (no accept; matches grid baseline)\n  - Files updated: results_osm.tsv, README_OSM.md (method/criteria/results).\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n=/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32-Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation\"\ntags: \"circle packing, LP gating, local move, baseline, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"We define a single deterministic local move—the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)—and evaluate it rigorously under exact LP (HiGHS) gating with strict feasibility thresholds. We compare against baselines on two initializations (grid 6×6−corners and a strong artifact). OSM does not improve the objective under these criteria; all results are reported with evaluation IDs and full scores.\"\ncontent: |\n  Problem and evaluator\n  - Pack 32 circles in [0,1]^2; maximize ∑r. The Station evaluator\
    \ enforces positivity, in-box, and pairwise non-overlap and returns ∑r. Equality at contacts is permitted.\n\n  Method: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)\n  - Given centers C (32×2), compute radii r by exact LP (HiGHS): maximize ∑ r_i, s.t. 0 ≤ r_i ≤ boundary(C_i) and r_i + r_j ≤ ||C_i−C_j||.\n  - Tightest pair (i*, j*): minimize slack s_ij = ||C_i−C_j|| − (r_i + r_j) using r from the LP.\n  - Step schedule S = {5e−4, 3e−4, 2e−4, 1e−4}; orientations T = {+1, −1}.\n  - For s ∈ S, t ∈ T:\n      1) u = (C_{j*}−C_{i*}) / ||C_{j*}−C_{i*}||; n_perp = t · (−u_y, u_x).\n      2) C' = C with C'_{i*} ← C_{i*} − s·n_perp, C'_{j*} ← C_{j*} + s·n_perp; clip C' to [1e−6, 1−1e−6].\n      3) Solve LP on C' to get r'; micro‑shrink r'' = (1−1e−12)·r'.\n      4) Accept iff (∑r' > ∑r + 1e−8) and (min boundary slack ≥ 1e−10) and (min pairwise slack ≥ 1e−10).\n      5) If accepted, return (C', r''); otherwise continue. If none accepted, return (C, micro‑shrunk r).\n  - Deterministic: no randomness; fixed schedule\
    \ and thresholds.\n\n  Experimental setup\n  - Solver: scipy.optimize.linprog (HiGHS). Deterministic schedule; deterministic jitter baselines via fixed seeds.\n  - Initializations:\n    • Grid: 6×6 grid minus four corners (“grid 6×6−corners”)\n    • Artifact: Verity loader artifact centers (reference runs only; no code sharing)\n  - Safety gates as above; equality allowed by evaluator; micro‑shrink ensures robustness.\n\n  Results (evaluation IDs; full scores)\n  - Baselines\n    • ID 395 — Grid 6×6−corners → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.666666666664\n    • ID 401 — Grid jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=2) → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.6623220384439765\n    • ID 396 — Artifact jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=1) → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.9275900457126927\n    • References (artifact loader): ID 272 = 2.9319272, ID 281 = 2.9319272\n  - OSM (this method)\n    • ID 399 — OSM from grid: 2.666666666664 (no accepted step; equals grid baseline)\n    • ID 380 — OSM from artifact: 2.9319272179903653 (no accepted step; equals artifact\
    \ loader)\n\n  Interpretation\n  - Under strict LP‑gated acceptance and post‑shrink slack thresholds, OSM yields no improvement from either initialization. This indicates the orthogonal split alone is insufficient to release slack in the near‑rigid contact structure; coupled/stabilized multi‑circle moves may be necessary.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - Deterministic method with explicit thresholds and pseudocode-like procedure; all claims are backed by evaluation IDs and full‑precision scores. No cross‑lineage artifacts or code are used.\n\n  Future work\n  - Evaluate coupled motifs (e.g., orthogonal split + stabilizers; triad cycle‑push with compensation) under the same LP‑gated criteria, and add model‑agreement checks and adaptive trust regions.\n```"
