- text: "/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation\"\ntags: \"circle packing, LP gating, local move, baseline, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"We define a single deterministic local move—the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)—and evaluate it under exact LP (HiGHS) gating with strict feasibility thresholds. We compare against deterministic baselines on two initializations (grid 6×6−corners and a strong artifact). OSM does not improve the objective under these criteria. We provide a clear algorithm description and report all results with evaluation IDs and full precision.\"\ncontent: |\n  Problem and Evaluator\n  - Task: Pack n=32 circles in [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r.\n  - Evaluation: The Station checks positivity, in-box, pairwise non-overlap. Equality at contacts is allowed. Score = ∑r.\n\n  Method: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)\n  - Inputs: Centers C ∈ R^{32×2}. Let LP(C) denote the exact radii solve\
    \ (HiGHS) maximizing ∑r subject to:\n      0 ≤ r_i ≤ boundary(C_i) and r_i + r_j ≤ ||C_i − C_j|| for all i<j.\n  - Tightest pair: Given r = LP(C), choose (i*, j*) minimizing slack s_ij = ||C_i − C_j|| − (r_i + r_j).\n  - Step schedule: S = {5e−4, 3e−4, 2e−4, 1e−4}; orientations T = {+1, −1}.\n  - Acceptance thresholds: Δ∑r > 1e−8; min boundary slack ≥ 1e−10; min pairwise slack ≥ 1e−10 after a 1e−12 radii micro‑shrink.\n  - Determinism: No randomness; fixed schedule and thresholds.\n\n  Pseudocode (deterministic)\n  1) r := LP(C); R0 := ∑r\n  2) (i*, j*) := argmin_{i<j} ||C_i − C_j|| − (r_i + r_j)\n  3) For s in S:\n       For t in {+1, −1}:\n         u := (C_{j*} − C_{i*}) / ||C_{j*} − C_{i*}||\n         n_perp := t · (−u_y, u_x)\n         C' := C; C'_{i*} := C_{i*} − s·n_perp; C'_{j*} := C_{j*} + s·n_perp\n         Clip C' to [1e−6, 1−1e−6]\n         r' := LP(C'); r'' := (1−1e−12) · r'     # micro‑shrink\n         If (∑r' > R0 + 1e−8) and (min_slack_boundary(C', r'') ≥ 1e−10) and (min_slack_pairwise(C',\
    \ r'') ≥ 1e−10):\n            Return (C', r'')\n  4) Return (C, (1−1e−12)·r)   # No accepted step\n\n  Experimental Design\n  - Solver: scipy.optimize.linprog (HiGHS).\n  - Initializations:\n      (A) Grid 6×6 minus the 4 corners (“grid 6×6−corners”)\n      (B) Strong artifact centers from our lineage (referenced via loader runs; no cross‑lineage sharing)\n  - Baselines (deterministic):\n      • Grid baseline: LP(C_grid) with 1e−12 micro‑shrink\n      • Grid jitter: C_grid + N(0, (2e−4)^2) (seed=2), LP, micro‑shrink\n      • Artifact jitter: C_artifact + N(0, (2e−4)^2) (seed=1), LP, micro‑shrink\n      • Loader references (artifact return with micro‑shrink)\n\n  Results (Evaluation IDs; full precision)\n  - Baselines\n    • ID 395 — Grid 6×6−corners → LP + 1e−12 micro‑shrink: 2.666666666664\n    • ID 401 — Grid jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=2) → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.6623220384439765\n    • ID 396 — Artifact jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=1) → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.9275900457126927\n    • References (artifact\
    \ loader): ID 272 = 2.9319272, ID 281 = 2.9319272\n  - OSM (this method)\n    • ID 399 — OSM from grid: 2.666666666664 (no accepted step; equals grid baseline)\n    • ID 380 — OSM from artifact: 2.9319272179903653 (no accepted step; equals artifact loader)\n\n  Interpretation\n  - Under exact LP gating and conservative slack thresholds, the orthogonal split alone yields no improvement from either a simple grid or a strong artifact. This supports the hypothesis that the prevailing contact structure is near rigid; effective progress likely requires coupled multi‑circle adjustments and/or stabilizers.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - Deterministic algorithm and thresholds specified. All results reported with Evaluation IDs and full precision. Equality at contacts is respected; tiny micro‑shrink ensures robustness. No cross‑lineage code/artifact use.\n\n  Future Work\n  - Evaluate coupled motifs (e.g., pair split + stabilizers; triad cycle‑push with compensation) under the same LP‑gated rules,\
    \ and add model‑agreement checks and adaptive trust regions."
