- text: "/execute_action{goto token_management}\n=/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"200-206\"\n    summary: |\n      Deterministic LP-gated local motifs; parity with artifact. \n      - Eval 375: Balanced boundary–neighbor push → 2.9319272 (parity).\n      - Eval 380: Orthogonal split on tightest pair (artifact) → 2.9319272 (parity).\n      - Storage/docs: results_375.tsv, CHANGELOG_201.md; meta updated; leaderboard checks.\n      Outcome: No safe improvement beyond artifact loader; policy unchanged (loader-only return; exact LP gating offline).\n\n  - ticks: \"207-218\"\n    summary: |\n      OSM (Orthogonal Split Motif) study setup; baselines and rigorous paper draft.\n      - Baselines: \n        • Eval 395: Grid 6×6−corners → LP + micro-shrink = 2.666666666664\n        • Eval 396: Artifact + deterministic jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=1) → 2.9275900457126927\n      - OSM from grid (repro ID): Eval 399 = 2.666666666664 (no accept).\n      - Storage/docs: results_osm.tsv,\
    \ README_OSM.md; Private memo on archive plan and IDs.\n      - Archive: Drafted OSM paper with method pseudocode, acceptance rules, baselines, and full-precision IDs.\n\n  - ticks: \"219-228\"\n    summary: |\n      OSM paper acceptance and coupled motif follow-up; triad motif evaluation.\n      - Archive: “OSM Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation” accepted (Capsule #9; 6/10). Addendum commits plan for a coupled triad motif.\n      - Triad motif (orthogonal split + nearest-third stabilizer), deterministic and LP-gated:\n        • Eval 406 (artifact init) = 2.9319272179903653 (parity).\n        • Eval 407 (grid init) = 2.666666666664 (parity).\n      - Storage/docs updated: results_osm.tsv, README_OSM.md, CHANGELOG_OSM_230.md; Archive addendum with IDs and interpretation.\n\n  - ticks: \"229-233\"\n    summary: |\n      Triad results recorded; deeper reflection; two-stabilizer motif plan and submissions.\n      - Results confirmed: Eval 406/407 parity; posted archive addendum.\n     \
    \ - Reflection (tick 232): challenged assumptions; designed two-stabilizer motif and model-agreement gating concept.\n      - Submissions (tick 233): Two-stabilizer motif from artifact and grid (LP-gated; loader/grid fallback); eval IDs pending at that time.\n      - Policy unchanged: loader-only return path; exact LP gating; strict safety thresholds; deterministic schedules; documentation maintained.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating pruning actions**\n\nI think it's better to first go and examine the list before deciding on any actions. I can propose pruning multiple ticks at once, summarizing them. However, I need to ensure we’re not pruning any protected or future ticks, particularly keeping an eye on tick 234. I previously pruned at tick 196, so it seems like we should be okay. I’ll follow through by checking token management, showing the list, and then proceeding with the prune.**Finalizing pruning actions**\n\nI see that we’ve already done many actions. It’s important to provide a comprehensive summary of potential blocks. Instead of 200-206, maybe we could prune 197-205 since we've already summarized some previously. I can also think about updating the Archive with a summary of our results, but we hit a snag with the Research Counter earlier. The user hasn’t asked for anything specific, so the best next step is to prune tokens. I’ll head over to token management to proceed with\
  \ that.**Considering token pruning**\n\nThe conversation seems to involve multiple steps from tick 197 to 234, and I can't know the exact lengths until I check Token Management. I can propose to prune from 205 to 234, but I need to include a summary, since consecutive blocks are accepted. However, I want to keep essential context for future reference. I can safely store previous results in the archive. It's clear I can't prune tick 234, but I can go up to 233. I must also watch out for any protected ticks.**Planning token pruning**\n\nI’m not exactly sure which ticks are currently protected, but I remember some earlier ones like 0, 1, 4, 5, 29, 52, and 48. I should check the previous \"Dialogue History,\" but for now, let's attempt to prune from 197 to 206. First, I'll head to token management to review the list before making any decisions. I can prune from 200 to 233, but not including 234. The prune actions are subject to cooldown, which I think was previously every 4 ticks. So, let’s\
  \ stick to the plan.**Crafting a summary for pruning**\n\nI want to create a thorough summary for ticks 200-233 before I prune. Here’s what I’ve got: For 200-206, there’s been balanced boundary-neighbor pushes and orthogonal splits, along with docs and meta updates. I’ll need to mention evaluation IDs 375, 380, and others. \n\nFor 207-218, we’re looking at final handoff and planning. Ticks 219-228 involve the OSM paper submission and triad evaluations. Finally, for 229-233, results recorded and two-stabilizer motifs submitted but still pending results. \n\nMy next step is to go to token management and prune these blocks accordingly."
