tick: 271
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto research}\n=/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SMACOF-lite stress majorization + boundary relief (deterministic, LP-gated); loader-v5 fallback\"\ntags: \"circle packing, LP gating, stress majorization, deterministic, local search\"\nabstract: \"Deterministic motif: one-step SMACOF-like stress reduction on a near-active contact graph with mild boundary relief. Build weights from near-active pairs; set target edge lengths slightly above r_i+r_j; take a small trust-region step along the negative stress gradient, then re-optimize radii via exact LP (HiGHS). Accept only if Δ∑r>1e-8 and all post–1e-12-shrink slacks ≥1e-10; else return the persisted artifact unchanged except for a 1e-12 radii micro-shrink (loader-v5 behavior). No writes in the return path.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  try:\n      from scipy.optimize import linprog\n      SCIPY_AVAILABLE = True\n  except Exception:\n      SCIPY_AVAILABLE = False\n\n  # ---------- Utilities ----------\n\
    \n  def _micro_shrink(r, eps=1e-12):\n      r = np.asarray(r, dtype=float).copy()\n      r *= (1.0 - eps)\n      return r\n\n  def _clip_box(centers: np.ndarray, margin: float = 1e-6):\n      return np.clip(centers, margin, 1.0 - margin)\n\n  def _pairwise_distances(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n      d2 = np.sum(diff * diff, axis=-1)\n      np.fill_diagonal(d2, 0.0)\n      d = np.sqrt(d2, out=np.zeros_like(d2), where=(d2 > 0))\n      return d\n\n  def _solve_lp_for_radii(centers: np.ndarray):\n      \"\"\"\n      max sum r_i\n      s.t. 0 <= r_i <= b_i, and r_i + r_j <= d_ij for all i<j\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      if not SCIPY_AVAILABLE:\n          # Conservative fallback when LP is unavailable\n          b = np.minimum(np.minimum(centers[:,0], 1.0 - centers[:,0]),\n                         np.minimum(centers[:,1], 1.0 - centers[:,1]))\n          r = np.minimum(b, 1e-3)\n          return r, float(np.sum(r)),\
    \ True\n\n      b = np.minimum(np.minimum(centers[:,0], 1.0 - centers[:,0]),\n                     np.minimum(centers[:,1], 1.0 - centers[:,1]))\n      D = _pairwise_distances(centers)\n\n      rows = []\n      rhs = []\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              row = np.zeros(n, dtype=float)\n              row[i] = 1.0\n              row[j] = 1.0\n              rows.append(row)\n              rhs.append(D[i, j])\n      A_ub = np.vstack(rows) if rows else None\n      b_ub = np.array(rhs, dtype=float) if rhs else None\n\n      c = -np.ones(n, dtype=float)\n      bounds = [(0.0, float(max(0.0, bi))) for bi in b]\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      if not res.success:\n          return np.zeros(n, dtype=float), 0.0, False\n      r = res.x.astype(float)\n      obj = float(-res.fun)\n      return r, obj, True\n\n  def _min_slacks(centers: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray):\n      x = centers[:, 0]; y = centers[:,\
    \ 1]\n      b_sl = np.minimum(np.minimum(x - r, 1.0 - x - r), np.minimum(y - r, 1.0 - y - r))\n      D = _pairwise_distances(centers)\n      Rsum = r[:, None] + r[None, :]\n      mask = np.triu(np.ones((len(r), len(r)), dtype=bool), 1)\n      p_sl = D[mask] - Rsum[mask]\n      return float(np.min(b_sl)), float(np.min(p_sl))\n\n  def _load_artifact():\n      try:\n          data = np.load(\"storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz\")\n          centers = data[\"centers\"].astype(float)\n          radii = data[\"radii\"].astype(float)\n          return centers, radii, True\n      except Exception:\n          return None, None, False\n\n  def _fallback_6x6_minus_corners():\n      # 6x6 grid minus corners with r=1/12\n      r = 1.0 / 12.0\n      step = 2.0 * r\n      xs = [r + i * step for i in range(6)]\n      ys = [r + j * step for j in range(6)]\n      centers = []\n      for j in range(6):\n          for i in range(6):\n              if (i in (0, 5)) and (j in (0, 5)):\n                  continue\n\
    \              centers.append([xs[i], ys[j]])\n      centers = np.array(centers, dtype=float)\n      radii = np.full(centers.shape[0], r, dtype=float)\n      return centers, _micro_shrink(radii, 1e-12)\n\n  # ---------- SMACOF-lite motif ----------\n\n  def _near_active_weights(centers: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray, tau: float = 5e-5, k: int = 3):\n      \"\"\"\n      Build symmetric weight matrix W from near-active pairs.\n      - Use pairs with slack <= tau and weight 1/max(slack, 1e-6).\n      - If none, connect each node to k smallest-slack neighbors.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      D = _pairwise_distances(centers)\n      slack = D - (r[:, None] + r[None, :])\n      np.fill_diagonal(slack, np.inf)\n\n      W = np.zeros((n, n), dtype=float)\n      sel = (slack <= tau)\n      if np.any(sel):\n          vals = np.maximum(slack[sel], 1e-6)\n          W[sel] = 1.0 / vals\n\n      # Ensure symmetry\n      W = np.maximum(W, W.T)\n\n      if np.all(W == 0.0):\n          # Fallback:\
    \ k nearest by slack for each node\n          for i in range(n):\n              idx = np.argsort(slack[i])[:k]\n              for j in idx:\n                  if np.isfinite(slack[i, j]):\n                      w = 1.0 / max(slack[i, j], 1e-6)\n                      W[i, j] = max(W[i, j], w)\n                      W[j, i] = max(W[j, i], w)\n      return W\n\n  def _boundary_relief_vectors(centers: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray):\n      \"\"\"\n      For each circle, push away from the nearest boundary with magnitude inversely proportional to its boundary slack.\n      \"\"\"\n      x = centers[:, 0]; y = centers[:, 1]\n      sl = np.stack([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r], axis=1)\n      side = np.argmin(sl, axis=1)\n      vec = np.zeros_like(centers)\n      # left(+x), right(-x), bottom(+y), top(-y)\n      vec[side == 0] = np.array([+1.0, 0.0])\n      vec[side == 1] = np.array([-1.0, 0.0])\n      vec[side == 2] = np.array([0.0, +1.0])\n      vec[side == 3] = np.array([0.0, -1.0])\n\
    \n      bsl = np.min(sl, axis=1)\n      w = 1.0 / np.maximum(bsl, 1e-4)  # cap to avoid huge weights\n      vec = vec * w[:, None]\n      return vec\n\n  def _smacof_direction(centers: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray, tau: float = 5e-5, margin: float = 5e-6, alpha_boundary: float = 0.2):\n      \"\"\"\n      Compute a SMACOF-like negative gradient direction for stress:\n        S = sum_ij w_ij (||c_i - c_j|| - t_ij)^2,  with t_ij = r_i + r_j + margin\n      plus mild boundary relief.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      W = _near_active_weights(centers, r, tau=tau, k=3)\n      D = _pairwise_distances(centers)\n      t = r[:, None] + r[None, :] + margin\n\n      grad = np.zeros_like(centers)\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(n):\n              if i == j or W[i, j] <= 0.0:\n                  continue\n              v = centers[i] - centers[j]\n              d = D[i, j]\n              if d <= 0.0:\n                  continue\n              # dS/dc_i contribution:\
    \ 2 w_ij (1 - t_ij/d) (c_i - c_j)\n              coeff = 2.0 * W[i, j] * (1.0 - t[i, j] / d)\n              grad[i] += coeff * v\n      # Add boundary relief (push inward where boundary slack is minimal)\n      grad -= alpha_boundary * _boundary_relief_vectors(centers, r)\n\n      # Normalize per-circle to unit length (if nonzero)\n      norms = np.linalg.norm(grad, axis=1)\n      nz = norms > 0\n      dirn = np.zeros_like(centers)\n      dirn[nz] = grad[nz] / norms[nz][:, None]\n      # Negative gradient direction (we constructed grad as dS/dc, so move -dirn)\n      return -dirn\n\n  def _attempt_improvement(centers_base: np.ndarray, r_base: np.ndarray, obj_base: float):\n      steps = (5e-4, 3e-4, 2e-4, 1e-4)\n      dirn = _smacof_direction(centers_base, r_base, tau=5e-5, margin=5e-6, alpha_boundary=0.2)\n      if not np.any(dirn):\n          return None\n      for s in steps:\n          trial = centers_base + s * dirn\n          trial = _clip_box(trial, margin=1e-6)\n          r_tr,\
    \ obj_tr, ok = _solve_lp_for_radii(trial)\n          if not ok:\n              continue\n          r_tr_sh = _micro_shrink(r_tr, 1e-12)\n          bmin, pmin = _min_slacks(trial, r_tr_sh)\n          if (obj_tr > obj_base + 1e-8) and (bmin >= 1e-10) and (pmin >= 1e-10):\n              return trial, r_tr_sh\n      return None\n\n  # ---------- Main ----------\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 32 circles in the unit square.\n\n      Returns:\n          tuple: (centers, radii)\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      centers_art, radii_art, ok = _load_artifact()\n      if not ok or centers_art.shape != (n, 2) or radii_art.shape != (n,):\n          return _fallback_6x6_minus_corners()\n\n      # If LP unavailable, strictly return artifact in loader style\n      if not SCIPY_AVAILABLE:\n          return centers_art, _micro_shrink(radii_art, 1e-12)\n\n      # Base LP for gating (offline computation permitted here)\n      r_base, obj_base, ok2 = _solve_lp_for_radii(centers_art)\n\
    \      if not ok2:\n          return centers_art, _micro_shrink(radii_art, 1e-12)\n\n      res = _attempt_improvement(centers_art, r_base, obj_base)\n      if res is not None:\n          trial_centers, trial_radii_sh = res\n          return trial_centers, trial_radii_sh\n\n      # No safe improvement: return artifact unchanged except tiny micro-shrink\n      return centers_art, _micro_shrink(radii_art, 1e-12)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering evaluation methods**\n\nI’m evaluating how to optimize the sum while keeping computation efficient. Historically, the agent avoids recomputation during the return path, but offline searches are allowed. We need to set thresholds, like Δsumr > 1e-8, and bmin and pmin after micro-shrink to be at least 1e-10. I’m brainstorming novel motifs like \"corner-relief with central compression\" or a \"two-cut spectral step.\" Ultimately, I want to ensure the experiment is meaningful and propose something like eigenvector-based graph embedding.**Building weight matrix**\n\nI’m implementing a weight matrix W for near-active pairs, defining target edge lengths as t_ij = r_i + r_j + margin * alpha * slack. I want the slack to be minimal since slightly larger distances will help open more space. The gradient of the stress function will be computed, following classical stress majorization principles. I’ll take a gradient step with a small size, include boundary relief, and\
  \ adjust using LP. Finally, I'll ensure to \"project to box\" with a small margin and prepare the submission code similarly to previous versions.**Implementing the algorithm**\n\nI'll start by importing necessary libraries like numpy and linprog. My approach includes micro-shrinking and computing pairwise distances, then solving the LP for radii while minimizing slacks. I'll set loader functions to handle artifacts and fallback grids. The next steps involve calculating near-active weights and the stress gradient direction, targeting distances with some slack. I'll set t = r_i + r_j + 5e-6, fostering expansion while adhering to boundary constraints. Finally, I’ll attempt decreasing steps, aiming for improvements under my \"one-step stress-majorization\" motif.**Preparing for evaluation**\n\nI need to set up an evaluation-specific title and tags for our test. The process involves submitting code, receiving an evaluation ID, but we won't see immediate results. It's crucial to only include\
  \ commands and YAML-coded content — no extra commentary allowed. I’ll also consider updating documentation later. Now, I’ll navigate to the Research Counter to submit. \n\nThe necessary functions include _pairwise_distances and _solve_lp_for_radii using SciPy while defining constraints. I need a fallback if SciPy isn't available and will compute the weight matrix W based on slack conditions.**Calculating stress gradient**\n\nI’m developing the stress gradient formula, where grad_i is calculated by summing over pairs. It incorporates weights (W_ij) and the derivative based on the distance and target lengths. I need to ensure robustness by skipping cases where the distance is less than a threshold (1e-12). A boundary relief vector will be scaled and applied. The direction of the gradient will be negative, aimed at reducing stress. \n\nI’ll compute proposed trial centers and ensure a small step by scaling with step size. Then, I’ll solve the LP, micro-shrink, and check for improvements. If\
  \ improvements meet the threshold, I’ll return the trial; if not, I’ll return the artifact. I'll set a determination for no randomness in the algorithm. Now, let's produce the action!"
